-- question. Apply after the chunks.
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A printer produces posters at a constant rate of 42 posters per minute. At what rate, in posters per hour, does the printer produce the posters?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["2520"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 2,520. There are 60 minutes in one hour. At a rate of 42 posters per minute, the number of posters produced in one hour can be determined by \((\frac{42\text{ posters}}{1\text{ minute}})(\frac{60\text{ minutes}}{1\text{ hour}})\) , which is 2,520 posters per hour.</p>' WHERE id='3c8fdc40';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A customer spent $27 to purchase oranges at $3 per pound. How many pounds of oranges did the customer purchase?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["9"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 9. It’s given that the customer spent $27 to purchase oranges at $3 per pound. Therefore, the number of pounds of oranges the customer purchased is \(\$27(\frac{1\text{ pound}}{\$3})\) , or 9 pounds.</p>' WHERE id='86636d8f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A mechanical device in a workshop produces items at a constant rate of 60 items per hour. At this rate, how many items will the mechanical device produce in 3 hours?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["180"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 180. It’s given that a mechanical device produces items at a constant rate of 60 items per hour. This rate can be written as</p><ul><li>\(\frac{60\text{ items}}{1\text{ hour}}\) . Let \(x\) represent the number of items the mechanical device will produce in 3 hours at the given rate. It follows that \(\frac{60\text{ items}}{1\text{ hour}}=\frac{x\text{ items}}{3\text{ hours}}\) , which can be written as \(\frac{60}{1}=\frac{x}{3}\) , or \(60 =\frac{x}{3}\) . Multiplying each side of this equation by 3 yields \(180 = x\). Therefore, at the given rate, the mechanical device will produce 180 items in 3 hours.</li></ul><p>Alternate approach: It''s given that a mechanical device produces items at a constant rate of 60 items per hour. At this rate, the mechanical device will produce \((\frac{60\text{ items}}{1\text{ hour}})(3\text{ hours})\), or 180 items in 3 hours.</p>' WHERE id='fe4c1c9e';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many fluid ounces are equivalent to 76 quarts? \((8\text{ fluid ounces} = 1\text{ cup and } 4\text{ cups} = 1\text{ quart})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["2432"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 2,432. It''s given that \(4\text{ cups} = 1\text{ quart}\). It follows that 76 quarts is equivalent to \((76\text{ quarts})(\frac{4\text{ cups}}{1\text{ quart}})\) , or 304 cups. It''s also given that \(8\text{ fluid ounces} = 1\text{ cup}\). It follows that 304 cups is equivalent to \((304\text{ cups})(\frac{8\text{ fluid ounces}}{1\text{ cup}})\) , or 2,432 fluid ounces.</p>' WHERE id='3310c2ab';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A square scale drawing has a side length of 55 inches, and 1 inch on the drawing represents an actual distance of 19 miles. A larger version of the same drawing is printed as a square with the side length 65% longer than the side length of the previous drawing. On the larger drawing, which of the following is closest to the actual distance, in miles, represented by 1 inch?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>6.65</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>11.52</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>31.35</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>35.75</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that 1 inch on the drawing represents an actual distance of 19 miles and that the larger drawing has a side length that is 65% longer than the side length of the previous drawing. It follows that \((1.65)(1\text{ inch})\), or 1.65 inches, on the larger drawing also represents an actual distance of 19 miles. Therefore, the scale of the larger drawing can be represented as \(\frac{19\text{ miles}}{1.65\text{ inches}}\) , which is approximately</p><ul><li>11.52 miles per inch. Therefore, 11.52 is closest to the actual distance, in miles, represented by 1 inch on the larger drawing.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='28ff3966';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The population density of Worthington is 290 people per square mile. Worthington has a population of 92,800 people. What is the area, in square miles, of Worthington?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>102,400</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>93,090</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>320</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>32</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It’s given that the population density of Worthington is 290 people per square mile and Worthington has a population of</p><ul><li>92,800 people. Therefore, the area of Worthington is \(92,800\text{ people }(\frac{1\text{ square mile}}{290\text{ people}})\) , which is equivalent to \(\frac{92,800\text{ square miles}}{290}\) , or 320 square miles.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='15617f62';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The number of raccoons in a 131 -square-mile area is estimated to be 2,358. What is the estimated population density, in raccoons per square mile, of this area?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>18</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>131</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>149</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>2,376</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that there are 2,358 raccoons in a 131 -square-mile area. The estimated population density, in raccoons per square mile, is the estimated number of raccoons divided by the number of square miles. Therefore, the estimated population density of this area is</p><ul><li>\(\frac{2,358\text{ raccoons}}{131\text{ square miles}}\) , or 18 raccoons per square mile.</li></ul><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the number of square miles in the area, not the estimated number of raccoons per square mile in this area.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='e3d49511';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A company designs and makes handbags. To estimate the mean weight of the handbags made by the company on a particular day, a sample of the handbags made by the company on that day was selected at random. Based on the sample, it is estimated that the mean weight of all handbags made by the company on that day is \(27.8\text{ ounces } (oZ)\) , with an associated margin of error of \(0.02 oZ\). Based on this estimate and associated margin of error, which of the following is the most plausible conclusion?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>The mean weight of all handbags made by the company on that day is between \\(27.78 oZ\\) and \\(27.82 oZ\\).</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>The actual weights of all handbags made by the company on that day are between \\(27.78 oZ\\) and \\(27.82 oZ\\).</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>The actual weights of all handbags from the sample are between \\(27.78 oZ\\) and \\(27.82 oZ\\).</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>The mean weight of all handbags made by the company on that day is \\(27.8 oZ\\).</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It''s given that the estimated mean weight of all handbags made by the company on a particular day is \(27.8 oZ\), with an associated margin of error of \(0.02 oZ\). It follows that plausible values for the mean weight are between \((27.8-0.02) oZ\) and (27.8</p><ul><li>\(+0.02) oZ\). Therefore, the most plausible conclusion is that the mean weight of all handbags made by the company on that day is between</li><li>\(27.78 oZ\) and \(27.82 oZ\).</li></ul><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='2c76bcce';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>During a study, the temperature, \(\text{ in degrees Celsius } (^{\circ}C)\) , of the air in a chamber was recorded to the nearest integer at certain times. The scatterplot shows the recorded temperature \(y\), \(\text{ in} ^{\circ}C\), of the air in the chamber \(x\) minutes after the start of the study.</p><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/e24765e6_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><h3>Prompt</h3><p>What was the average rate of change, \(\text{ in} ^{\circ}C\) per minute, of the recorded temperature of the air in the chamber from \(x = 5\) to \(x = 7\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["5"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 5. For the graph shown, \(x\) represents time, in minutes, and \(y\) represents temperature, in degrees Celsius \((^{\circ}C)\) . Therefore, the average rate of change, in \(^{\circ}C\) per minute, of the recorded temperature of the air in the chamber between two x-values is the difference in the corresponding y-values divided by the difference in the x-values. The graph shows that at \(x = 5\), the corresponding y-value is 14. The graph also shows that at \(x = 7\), the corresponding y-value is 24. It follows that the average rate of change, in \(^{\circ}C\) per minute, from \(x = 5\) to \(x = 7\) is \(\frac{24-14}{7-5}\) , which is equivalent to \(\frac{10}{2}\) , or 5.</p>' WHERE id='e24765e6';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A special camera is used for underwater ocean research. When the camera is at a depth of 58 fathoms, what is the camera''s depth in feet? \((1\text{ fathom} = 6\text{ feet})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["348"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 348. It''s given that 1 fathom is equivalent to 6 feet. Therefore, 58 fathoms is equivalent to \((58\text{ fathoms})(\frac{6\text{ feet}}{1\text{ fathom}})\) , or 348 feet. Thus, when the camera is at a depth of 58 fathoms, the camera''s depth, in feet, is 348.</p>' WHERE id='2d16d62c';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>A sample of oak has a density of 807 kilograms per cubic meter. The sample is in the shape of a cube, where each edge has a length of</p><h3>Prompt</h3><ul><li>0.90 meters. To the nearest whole number, what is the mass, in kilograms, of this sample?</li></ul>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>588</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>726</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>897</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>1,107</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that the sample is in the shape of a cube with edge lengths of 0.9 meters. Therefore, the volume of the sample is</p><ul><li>\(0.90^{3}\), or 0.729, cubic meters. It’s also given that the sample has a density of 807 kilograms per 1 cubic meter. Therefore, the mass of this sample is \(0.729\text{ cubic meters }(\frac{807\text{ kilograms}}{1\text{ cubic meter}})\) , or 588.303 kilograms. Rounding this mass to the nearest whole number gives 588 kilograms. Therefore, to the nearest whole number, the mass, in kilograms, of this sample is 588.</li></ul><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='69f6717f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many yards are equivalent to 612 inches? \((1\text{ yard} = 36\text{ inches})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0.059</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>17</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>576</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>22,032</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It’s given that \(1\text{ yard} = 36\text{ inches}\). Therefore, 612 inches is equivalent to \(612\text{ inches }(\frac{1\text{ yard}}{36\text{ inches}})\) , which can be rewritten as \(\frac{612\text{ yards}}{36}\) , or 17 yards.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the number of yards that are equivalent to 2.124 inches.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the number of yards that are equivalent to 20,736 inches.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the number of yards that are equivalent to 793,152 inches.</p>' WHERE id='aeeaec96';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The speed of a vehicle is increasing at a rate of 7.3 meters per second squared. What is this rate, in miles per minute squared, rounded to the nearest tenth? <img class="minl" src="/qimg/e7133228_m0.webp" alt=""></p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0.3</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>16.3</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>195.8</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>220.4</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that the speed of a vehicle is increasing at a rate of 7.3 meters per second squared. It''s given to use \(1\text{ mile}\)</p><ul><li>\(= 1,609\text{ meters}\). There are 60 seconds in 1 minute; therefore, \(60^{2}\) or 3,600 seconds squared is equal to 1 minute squared. It follows that the rate of 7.3 meters per second squared is equivalent to \((\frac{7.3\text{ meters}}{1\text{ second squared}})(\frac{1\text{ mile}}{1,609\text{ meters}})(\frac{3,600\text{ seconds squared}}{1\text{ minute squared}})\) , or approximately</li><li>\(16.33\text{ miles per minute squared}\). The rate, in miles per minute squared, rounded to the nearest tenth is 16.3.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='e7133228';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An object has a mass of 168 grams and a volume of 24 cubic centimeters. What is the density, in grams per cubic centimeter, of the object?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>7</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>144</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>192</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>4,032</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It''s given that the object has a mass of 168 grams and a volume of 24 cubic centimeters. Dividing the mass, in grams, of the object by the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the object gives the density, in grams per cubic centimeter, of the object. It follows that the density of the object is \(\frac{168\text{ grams}}{24\text{ cubic centimeters}}\) , which is equivalent to \(\frac{168}{24}\) grams per cubic centimeter, or 7 grams per cubic centimeter.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='1b403590';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A giant armadillo has a mass of 39 kilograms. What is the giant armadillo''s mass in grams? \((1\text{ kilogram}= 1,000\text{ grams})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["39000"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 39,000. It’s given that the giant armadillo has a mass of 39 kilograms. Since 1 kilogram is equal to 1,000 grams, 39 kilograms is equal to \(39\text{ kilograms } (\frac{1,000\text{ grams}}{1\text{ kilogram}})\) , or 39,000 grams. Therefore, the giant armadillo’s mass, in grams, is 39,000.</p>' WHERE id='c81499e1';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many yards are equivalent to 77 rods? \((5.5\text{ yards} = 1\text{ rod})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["423.5, 847/2"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 423.5. It''s given that \(5.5\text{ yards} = 1\text{ rod}\). Therefore, 77 rods is equivalent to \((77\text{ rods})(\frac{5.5\text{ yards}}{1\text{ rod}})\) , or 423.5 yards. Note that 423.5 and 847/2 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='0700a2d5';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A cherry pitting machine pits 12 pounds of cherries in 3 minutes. At this rate, how many minutes does it take the machine to pit 96 pounds of cherries?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>8</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>15</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>24</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>36</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It''s given that the cherry pitting machine pits 12 pounds of cherries in 3 minutes. This rate can be written as</p><ul><li>\(\frac{12\text{ pounds of cherries}}{3\text{ minutes}}\) . If the number of minutes it takes the machine to pit 96 pounds of cherries is represented by \(x\), the value of \(x\) can be calculated by solving the equation \(\frac{12\text{ pounds of cherries}}{3\text{ minutes}}=\frac{96\text{ pounds of cherries}}{x\text{ minutes}}\) , which can be rewritten as \(\frac{12}{3}=\frac{96}{x}\) , or \(4 =\frac{96}{x}\) . Multiplying each side of this equation by \(x\) yields \(4x = 96\). Dividing each side of this equation by 4 yields \(x = 24\). Therefore, it takes the machine 24 minutes to pit 96 pounds of cherries.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the number of minutes it takes the machine to pit 32, not 96, pounds of cherries.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the number of minutes it takes the machine to pit 60, not 96, pounds of cherries.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the number of minutes it takes the machine to pit 144, not 96, pounds of cherries.</p>' WHERE id='fe1ec415';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A kangaroo has a mass of 28 kilograms. What is the kangaroo''s mass, in grams? \((1\text{ kilogram}= 1,000\text{ grams})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>28,000</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>1,028</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>972</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>784</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It''s given that a kangaroo has a mass of 28 kilograms and that 1 kilogram is equal to 1,000 grams. Therefore, the kangaroo''s mass, in grams, is \(28\text{ kilograms }(\frac{1,000\text{ grams}}{1\text{ kilogram}})\) , which is equivalent to 28,000 grams.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='ba62b0b0';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An object travels at a constant speed of 12 centimeters per second. At this speed, what is the time, in seconds, that it would take for the object to travel 108 centimeters?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>9</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>96</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>120</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>972</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. If the object travels 108 centimeters at a speed of 12 centimeters per second, the time of travel can be determined by dividing the total distance by the speed. This results in \(\frac{108\text{ centimeters}}{12\text{ centimeters}/\text{ second}}\) , which is 9 seconds.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='7cd1c6db';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many feet are equivalent to 34 yards? \((1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["102"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 102. It’s given that 1 yard is equivalent to 3 feet. Therefore, 34 yards is equivalent to \((34\text{ yards})(\frac{3\text{ feet}}{1\text{ yard}})\) , or 102 feet.</p>' WHERE id='d0d9ede4';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A distance of 61 furlongs is equivalent to how many feet? \((1\text{ furlong} = 220\text{ yards and } 1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["40260"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 40,260. It''s given that \(1\text{ furlong} = 220\text{ yards}\) and \(1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet}\). It follows that a distance of 61 furlongs is equivalent to \((61\text{ furlongs})(\frac{220\text{ yards}}{1\text{ furlong}})(\frac{3\text{ feet}}{1\text{ yard}})\) , or 40,260 feet.</p>' WHERE id='ec787383';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A fish swam a distance of 5,104 yards. How far did the fish swim, in miles? \((1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0.3</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>2.9</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>3,344</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>6,864</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It’s given that the fish swam 5,104 yards and that 1 mile is equal to 1,760 yards. Therefore, the fish swam</p><ul><li>\(5,104\text{ yards }(\frac{1\text{ mile}}{1,760\text{ yards}})\) , which is equivalent to \(\frac{5,104}{1,760}\text{ miles}\), or 2.9 miles.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='85b33aa8';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The population density of Cedar County is 230 people per square mile. The county has a population of 85,100 people. What is the area, in square miles, of Cedar County?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["370"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 370. It’s given that the population density of Cedar County is 230 people per square mile and the county has a population of 85,100 people. Based on the population density, it follows that the area of Cedar County is \((85,100\text{ people})(\frac{1\text{ square mile}}{230\text{ people}})\) , or 370 square miles.</p>' WHERE id='873d2838';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What length, in centimeters, is equivalent to a length of 51 meters? \((1\text{ meter} = 100\text{ centimeters})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0.051</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>0.51</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>5,100</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>51,000</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. Since 1 meter is equal to 100 centimeters, 51 meters is equal to \(51\text{ meters }(\frac{100\text{ centimeters}}{1\text{ meter}})\) , or 5,100 centimeters.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from dividing, rather than multiplying, 51 by 100.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the length, in millimeters rather than centimeters, that is equivalent to a length of 51 meters.</p>' WHERE id='2cdefcb1';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A certain town has an area of 4.36 square miles. What is the area, in square yards, of this town? \((1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>404</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>7,674</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>710,459</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>13,505,536</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. Since the number of yards in 1 mile is 1,760, the number of square yards in 1 square mile is (1,760)(1,760) = 3,097,600. Therefore, if the area of the town is 4.36 square miles, it is 4.36(3,097,600) = 13,505,536, in square yards.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from dividing the number of yards in a mile by the square mileage of the town.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from multiplying the number of yards in a mile by the square mileage of the town.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from dividing the number of square yards in a square mile by the square mileage of the town.</p>' WHERE id='c7c6445f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A distance of 112 furlongs is equivalent to how many feet? \((1\text{ furlong} = 220\text{ yards and } 1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["73920"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 73,920. It''s given that \(1\text{ furlong} = 220\text{ yards}\) and \(1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet}\). It follows that a distance of 112 furlongs is equivalent to \((112\text{ furlongs})(\frac{220\text{ yards}}{1\text{ furlong}})(\frac{3\text{ feet}}{1\text{ yard}})\) , or 73,920 feet.</p>' WHERE id='73ddfdac';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The function \(f(w) = 6w^{2}\) gives the area of a rectangle, \(\text{ in square feet } (ft^{2})\) , if its width is \(w ft\) and its length is 6 times its width. Which of the following is the best interpretation of \(f(14) = 1,176\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>If the width of the rectangle is \\(14 ft\\), then the area of the rectangle is \\(1,176 ft^{2}\\) .</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>If the width of the rectangle is \\(14 ft\\), then the length of the rectangle is \\(1,176 ft\\).</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>If the width of the rectangle is \\(1,176 ft\\), then the length of the rectangle is \\(14 ft\\).</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>If the width of the rectangle is \\(1,176 ft\\), then the area of the rectangle is \\(14 ft^{2}\\).</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. The function \(f\) gives the area of the rectangle, in \(ft^{2}\) , if its width is \(w ft\). Since the value of \(f(14)\) is the value of \(f(w)\) if \(w = 14\), it follows that \(f(14)= 1,176\) means that \(f(w)\) is 1,176 if \(w = 14\). In the given context, this means that if the width of the rectangle is \(14 ft\), then the area of the rectangle is \(1,176 ft^{2}\) .</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from interpreting \(f(w)\) as the width, in \(ft\), of the rectangle if its area is \(w ft^{2}\) , rather than as the area, in \(ft^{2}\) , of the rectangle if its width is \(w ft\).</p>' WHERE id='67f4b449';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m10.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>The expression above is equivalent to <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m0.webp" alt="">, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of b?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["5/2"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m1.webp" alt="">. The expression <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m2.webp" alt=""> can be written in the form <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m3.webp" alt="">, where a, b, and c are</p><p>constants, by multiplying out the expression using the distributive property of multiplication over addition. The result is</p><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m4.webp" alt="">. This expression can be rewritten by multiplying as indicated to give</p><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m5.webp" alt="">, which can be simplified to <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m6.webp" alt="">, or <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m7.webp" alt="">. This is in the form <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m8.webp" alt="">,</p><p>where the value of b is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a391ed22_m9.webp" alt="">. Note that 5/2 and 2.5 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='a391ed22';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(m(t)=-0.0274(\frac{t}{7})^{2}+7.3873(\frac{t}{7})+75.032\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>The function \(m\) gives the predicted body mass \(m(t)\), in kilograms (kg) , of a certain animal \(t\) days after it was born in a wildlife reserve, where \(t \le 390\). Which of the following is the best interpretation of the statement “\(m(330)\) is approximately equal to 362” in this context?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>The predicted body mass of the animal was approximately \\(330 kg 362\\) days after it was born.</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>The predicted body mass of the animal was approximately \\(362 kg 330\\) days after it was born.</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>The predicted body mass of the animal was approximately \\(362 kg\\frac{330}{7}\\) days after it was born.</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>The predicted body mass of the animal was approximately \\(\\frac{330}{7}kg 362\\) days after it was born.</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. In the statement "\(m(330)\) is approximately equal to 362," the input of the function, 330, is the value of \(t\), the elapsed time, in days, since the animal was born. The approximate value of the function, 362, is the predicted body mass, in kilograms, of the animal after that time has elapsed. Therefore, the predicted body mass of the animal was approximately \(362 kg 330\) days after it was born.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This would be the best interpretation of the statement "\(m(362)\) is approximately equal to 330."</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. The number \(\frac{330}{7}\) is the number of weeks, not the number of days, after the animal was born.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This would be the best interpretation of the statement "\(m(362)\) is approximately equal to \(\frac{330}{7}\) ."</p>' WHERE id='52e589f9';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m8.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>If <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m0.webp" alt=""> satisfies the system of equations above, what is the value of y ?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["3/2"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m1.webp" alt="">. One method for solving the system of equations for y is to add corresponding sides of the two equations. Adding the</p><p>left-hand sides gives <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m2.webp" alt="">, or 4y. Adding the right-hand sides yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m3.webp" alt="">. It follows that <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m4.webp" alt="">. Finally, dividing</p><p>both sides of <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m5.webp" alt=""> by 4 yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m6.webp" alt=""> or <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d1b66ae6_m7.webp" alt="">. Note that 3/2 and 1.5 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='d1b66ae6';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/1a1a95de_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/1a1a95de_m0.webp" alt=""></p><p>\(e\) <img class="minl" src="/qimg/1a1a95de_m1.webp" alt=""></p><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/1a1a95de_m2.webp" alt=""> <img class="minl" src="/qimg/1a1a95de_m3.webp" alt=""> \(O\) <img class="minl" src="/qimg/1a1a95de_m4.webp" alt=""> <img class="minl" src="/qimg/1a1a95de_m5.webp" alt=""> \(m\) \(n\) <img class="minl" src="/qimg/1a1a95de_m6.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>A certain open star cluster contains M-type stars and K-type stars. The estimated total mass of M-type and K-type stars in this open star cluster is \(127,882\text{ quettagrams}\). The graph shown models the possible combinations of the number of M-type stars, \(x\), and K-type stars, \(y\), that could be in this open star cluster if all the M-type stars have the same estimated mass and all the K-type stars have the same estimated mass. Based on the graph, which of the following is closest to the estimated mass, in quettagrams, of each M-type star in this cluster?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>811</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>938</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>51,904</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>75,978</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. The x-intercept of the graph shown represents the situation in which the cluster contains only M-type stars. Based on the graph, the x-intercept is approximately (158,0) . Therefore, if there are 0 K-type stars in the cluster, then there are approximately 158 M-type stars in the cluster. It’s given that the total mass of the stars in this cluster is 127,882 quettagrams. Therefore, the estimated mass of each M- type star is approximately \(\frac{127,882}{158}\) quettagrams, or approximately 809.38 quettagrams. Thus, of the given choices, 811 is the closest to the estimated mass, in quettagrams, of each M-type star in this cluster.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value closest to the estimated mass, in quettagrams, of each K-type star, not each M-type star, in this cluster.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='1a1a95de';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/9b0a4eae_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>The graph in the xy-plane models the possible combinations of length \(x\), in meters (m) , and width \(y\), in meters, for a rectangle with a perimeter of \(36 m\). Which statement is the best interpretation of the point (8,10) in this context?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>The length is \\(10 m\\) less than the perimeter, and the width is \\(8 m\\) less than the perimeter.</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>The length is \\(10 m\\), and the width is \\(8 m\\).</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>The length is \\(8 m\\), and the width is \\(10 m\\).</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>The length is \\(8 m\\) less than the perimeter, and the width is \\(10 m\\) less than the perimeter.</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It’s given that the graph in the xy-plane models the possible combinations of length \(x\), in meters (m), and width \(y\), in meters, for a rectangle with a perimeter of 36 m. Since \(x\) represents the length, in meters, and \(y\) represents the width, in meters, the point (8,10) in the xy-plane represents a rectangle whose length is 8 m and whose width is 10 m.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is an interpretation of the point (10,8), not (8,10) .</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>' WHERE id='9b0a4eae';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>On a car trip, Rhett and Jessica each drove for part of the trip, and the total distance they drove was under 220 miles. Rhett drove at an average speed of \(35\text{ miles per hour } (mph)\) , and Jessica drove at an average speed of \(40 mph\). Which of the following inequalities represents this situation, where \(r\) is the number of hours Rhett drove and \(j\) is the number of hours Jessica drove?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(35r+ 40j &gt; 220\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(35r+ 40j &lt; 220\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(40r+ 35j &gt; 220\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(40r+ 35j &lt; 220\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It’s given that Rhett drove at an average speed of 35 miles per hour and that he drove for \(r\) hours. Multiplying 35 miles per hour by \(r\) hours yields \(35r\) miles, or the distance that Rhett drove. It’s also given that Jessica drove at an average speed of 40 miles per hour and that she drove for \(j\) hours. Multiplying 40 miles per hour by \(j\) hours yields \(40j\) miles, or the distance that Jessica drove. The total distance, in miles, that Rhett and Jessica drove can be represented by the expression \(35r+ 40j\). It’s given that the total distance they drove was under 220 miles. Therefore, the inequality \(35r+ 40j &lt; 220\) represents this situation.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This inequality represents a situation in which the total distance Rhett and Jessica drove was over, rather than under, 220 miles.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This inequality represents a situation in which Rhett drove at an average speed of 40, rather than 35, miles per hour, Jessica drove at an average speed of 35, rather than 40, miles per hour, and the total distance they drove was over, rather than under, 220 miles.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This inequality represents a situation in which Rhett drove at an average speed of 40, rather than 35, miles per hour, and Jessica drove at an average speed of 35, rather than 40, miles per hour.</p>' WHERE id='ee439cff';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(y \le x + 7\)</p><p>\(y \ge -2x - 1\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>Which point \((x,y)\) is a solution to the given system of inequalities in the xy-plane?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>(-14,0)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>(0,-14)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>(0,14)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>(14,0)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. A point \((x,y)\) is a solution to a system of inequalities in the xy-plane if substituting the x-coordinate and the y-coordinate of the point for \(x\) and \(y\), respectively, in each inequality makes both of the inequalities true. Substituting the x-coordinate and the y-coordinate of choice D, 14 and 0, for \(x\) and \(y\), respectively, in the first inequality in the given system, \(y \le x + 7\), yields 0 ≤ 14+ 7, or 0 ≤ 21, which is true. Substituting 14 for \(x\) and 0 for \(y\) in the second inequality in the given system, \(y \ge -2x - 1\), yields 0≥ -2(14)- 1, or 0 ≥ -29, which is true. Therefore, the point (14,0) is a solution to the given system of inequalities in the xy-plane.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Substituting -14 for \(x\) and 0 for \(y\) in the inequality \(y \le x + 7\) yields 0 ≤ -14+ 7, or 0 ≤ -7, which is not true.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Substituting 0 for \(x\) and -14 for \(y\) in the inequality \(y \ge -2x - 1\) yields -14 ≥-2(0) - 1, or -14 ≥ -1, which is not true.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. Substituting 0 for \(x\) and 14 for \(y\) in the inequality \(y \le x + 7\) yields 14 ≤ 0 + 7, or 14 ≤ 7, which is not true.</p>' WHERE id='541bef2f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The pressure exerted on a scuba diver at sea level is \(14.70\text{ pounds per square inch } (psi)\). For each foot the scuba diver descends below sea level, the pressure exerted on the scuba diver increases by \(0.44 psi\). What is the total pressure, in \(psi\), exerted on the scuba diver at 105 feet below sea level?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>60.90</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>31.50</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>14.70</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>0.44</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It''s given that the pressure exerted on a scuba diver at sea level is \(14.70\text{ pounds per square inch } (psi)\) . It''s also given that for each foot the scuba diver descends below sea level, the pressure exerted on the scuba diver increases by \(0.44 psi\). The total pressure, in \(psi\), exerted on the scuba diver at \(x\) feet below sea level can be represented by the expression \(0.44x + 14.70\). Substituting 105 for \(x\) in this expression yields 0.44(105) + 14.70, or 60.90. Therefore, the total pressure exerted on the scuba diver at 105 feet below sea level is</p><ul><li>\(60.90 psi\).</li></ul><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the pressure, in \(psi\), exerted on the scuba diver at sea level, not at 105 feet below sea level.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the rate by which the pressure, in \(psi\), exerted on the scuba diver increases for each foot the scuba diver descends below sea level.</p>' WHERE id='1c29bfd1';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A bus traveled on the highway and on local roads to complete a trip of \(160\text{ miles}\). The trip took \(4\text{ hours}\). The bus traveled at an average speed of \(55\text{ miles per hour } (mph)\) on the highway and an average speed of \(25 mph\) on local roads. If \(x\) is the time, in hours, the bus traveled on the highway and \(y\) is the time, in hours, it traveled on local roads, which system of equations represents this situation?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(55x + 25y = 4\\) \\(x + y = 160\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(55x + 25y = 160\\) \\(x + y = 4\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(25x + 55y = 4\\) \\(x + y = 160\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(25x + 55y = 160\\) \\(x + y = 4\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. If the bus traveled at an average speed of \(55\text{ miles per hour } (mph)\) on the highway for \(x\) hours, then the bus traveled \(55x\) miles on the highway. If the bus traveled at an average speed of \(25 mph\) on local roads for \(y\) hours, then the bus traveled \(25y\) miles on local roads. It''s given that the trip was 160 miles. This can be represented by the equation \(55x + 25y = 160\). It''s also given that the trip took 4 hours. This can be represented by the equation \(x + y = 4\). Therefore, the system consisting of the equations \(55x + 25y = 160\) and \(x + y = 4\) represents this situation.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This system of equations represents a situation where the trip was 4 miles and took 160 hours.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This system of equations represents a situation where the trip was 4 miles and took 160 hours, and the bus traveled at an average speed of \(25 mph\) on the highway and \(55 mph\) on local roads.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This system of equations represents a situation where the bus traveled at an average speed of \(25 mph\) on the highway and \(55 mph\) on local roads.</p>' WHERE id='74c03c21';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A line segment that has a length of \(115\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) is divided into three parts. One part is \(47 cm\) long. The other two parts have lengths that are equal to each other. What is the length, in \(cm\), of one of the other two parts of equal length?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["34"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 34. It’s given that a line segment has a length of \(115 cm\) and is divided into three parts, where one part is \(47 cm\) long and the other two parts have lengths that are equal. If \(x\) represents the length, in cm, of each of the two parts of equal length, then the equation 47 \(+ x + x = 115\), or \(47 + 2x = 115\), represents this situation. Subtracting 47 from each side of this equation yields \(2x = 68\). Dividing each side of this equation by 2 yields \(x = 34\). Therefore, the length, in cm, of one of the two parts of equal length is 34.</p>' WHERE id='79cf8505';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A batch of banana milkshakes consists of 4 cups of ice cream and 2 bananas and has \(1,114\text{ milligrams } (mg)\) of calcium. There is \(276 mg\) of calcium in 1 cup of the ice cream used to make this batch of milkshakes. How much calcium, in mg, is in 1 banana?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>5</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>10</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>419</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>1,104</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that a batch of banana milkshakes consists of 4 cups of ice cream and 2 bananas and has 1,114 mg of calcium. It’s also given that there is 276 mg of calcium in 1 cup of the ice cream used to make this batch of milkshakes. It follows that the total amount of calcium in 4 cups of ice cream is 4(276) , or 1,104 mg. Let \(x\) represent the amount of calcium, in mg, in 1 banana. It follows that the total amount of calcium in 2 bananas is \(2x\) mg. Since the batch of banana milkshakes has 1,114 mg of calcium, the equation \(1,104 + 2x = 1,114\) represents this situation. Subtracting 1,104 from both sides of this equation yields \(2x = 10\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 2 yields \(x\) = 5. Therefore, the amount of calcium in 1 banana is 5 mg.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the amount of calcium, in mg, in 2 bananas, not in 1 banana.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the amount of calcium, in mg, in 4 cups of ice cream, not in 1 banana.</p>' WHERE id='0d1b1e35';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(f(x) = 2x+244\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>The given function \(f\) represents the perimeter, in centimeters (cm), of a rectangle with a length of \(x cm\) and a fixed width. What is the width, in \(cm\), of the rectangle?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>2</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>122</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>244</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>488</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that \(f(x) = 2x+244\) represents the perimeter, in centimeters (cm), of a rectangle with a length of \(x cm\) and a fixed width. If \(w\) represents a fixed width, in \(cm\), then the perimeter, in \(cm\), of a rectangle with a length of \(x cm\) and a fixed width of \(w cm\) can be given by the function \(f(x) = 2x+2w\). Therefore, \(2x + 2w = 2x + 244\). Subtracting \(2x\) from both sides of this equation yields \(2w = 244\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 2 yields \(w = 122\). Therefore, the width, in \(cm\), of the rectangle is 122.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='c8fb6bcb';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In science class, Diego conducted an experiment to learn about evaporation. Diego measured the height of fluid in a beaker over a period of time. The function \(f(x) = 39-0.18x\) gives the estimated height, in centimeters (cm) , of the fluid in the beaker \(x\) days after the start of the experiment. Which of the following is the best interpretation of 39 in this context?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<p>The estimated height, in cm, of the fluid at the start of the experiment</p>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<p>The estimated height, in cm, of the fluid at the end of the experiment</p>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<p>The estimated change in the height, in cm, of the fluid each day</p>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<p>The estimated number of days for all the fluid to evaporate</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that the function \(f(x) = 39-0.18x\) gives the estimated height, in centimeters (cm), of the fluid in the beaker \(x\) days after the start of the experiment. For a function defined by an equation of the form \(f(x) = b+mx\), where \(m\) and \(b\) are constants, \(b\) represents the value of \(f(x)\) when \(x = 0\). It follows that in the given function, 39 represents the value of \(f(x)\) when \(x = 0\). Since \(x = 0\) represents the start of the experiment, then the best interpretation of 39 in this context is that the estimated height, in cm, of the fluid was 39 at the start of the experiment.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. The estimated change in the height, in cm, of the fluid each day is 0.18, not 39.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>' WHERE id='9d9fe1e6';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Each side of a 30-sided polygon has one of three lengths. The number of sides with length \(8\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) is 5 times the number of sides \(n\) with length \(3 cm\). There are 6 sides with length \(4 cm\). Which equation must be true for the value of \(n\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(5n + 6 = 30\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(6n + 6 = 30\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(8n + 3n + 4n = 30\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(8(5n) +3n+4(6) = 30\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It’s given that each side of a 30-sided polygon has one of three lengths. It''s also given that the number of sides with length 8 centimeters (cm) is 5 times the number of sides \(n\) with length \(3 cm\). Therefore, there are \(5 \times n\), or \(5n\), sides with length \(8 cm\). It’s also given that there are 6 sides with length \(4 cm\). Therefore, the number of \(3 cm\), \(4 cm\), and \(8 cm\) sides are \(n\), 6, and \(5n\), respectively. Since there are a total of 30 sides, the equation \(n + 6 + 5n = 30\) represents this situation. Combining like terms on the left-hand side of this equation yields \(6n\) + 6 = 30. Therefore, the equation that must be true for the value of \(n\) is \(6n + 6 = 30\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='0cb57740';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A museum rents tablets to visitors. The museum earns revenue of $14 for each tablet rented for the day. On Wednesday, the museum earned $406 in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of $112. How many tablets did the museum rent on Wednesday? <img class="minl" src="/qimg/feb78194_m1.webp" alt=""></p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["37"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 37. It''s given that the museum earns revenue of $14 for each tablet rented for the day. It''s also given that on Wednesday, the museum earned $406 in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of $112. Let \(x\) represent the number of tablets the museum rented on Wednesday. It follows that the total revenue can be represented by the expression \(14x\). Because \(\text{ profit} =\text{ total revenue}\) <img class="minl" src="/qimg/feb78194_m0.webp" alt="">, the equation \(406 = 14x - 112\) represents this situation. Adding 112 to both sides of this equation yields \(14x = 518\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 14 yields \(x = 37\). Therefore, the museum rented 37 tablets on Wednesday.</p>' WHERE id='feb78194';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/466b87e3_m6.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>In the system of equations above, c is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of c ?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["1/2"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/466b87e3_m0.webp" alt="">. A system of two linear equations has no solution when the graphs of the equations have the same slope and</p><p>different y-intercepts. Each of the given linear equations is written in the slope-intercept form, <img class="minl" src="/qimg/466b87e3_m1.webp" alt="">, where m is the slope and b is the</p><p>y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph of the equation. For these two linear equations, the y-intercepts are <img class="minl" src="/qimg/466b87e3_m2.webp" alt=""> and <img class="minl" src="/qimg/466b87e3_m3.webp" alt="">. Thus, if the</p><p>system of equations has no solution, the slopes of the graphs of the two linear equations must be the same. The slope of the graph of the first</p><p>linear equation is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/466b87e3_m4.webp" alt="">. Therefore, for the system of equations to have no solution, the value of c must be <img class="minl" src="/qimg/466b87e3_m5.webp" alt="">. Note that 1/2 and .5 are examples</p><p>of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='466b87e3';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A city employee will plant two types of bushes, azaleas and boxwoods, in a park. There will be no more than 164 total bushes planted, and the number of azaleas planted will be at most three times the number of boxwoods planted. Which of the following systems of inequalities best represents this situation, where \(a\) is the number of azaleas that will be planted, and \(b\) is the number of boxwoods that will be planted?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<p>\\(a+ b \\ge 164\\) \\(3a \\ge b\\)</p>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<p>\\(a+ b \\ge 164\\) \\(a \\le 3b\\)</p>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<p>\\(a+ b \\le 164\\) \\(3a \\ge b\\)</p>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<p>\\(a+ b \\le 164\\) \\(a \\le 3b\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It’s given that a city employee will plant azaleas and boxwoods in a park, where \(a\) is the number of azaleas that will be planted and \(b\) is the number of boxwoods that will be planted. It’s also given that there will be no more than 164 total bushes planted, which can be represented by the inequality \(a+ b \le 164\). It’s also given that the number of azaleas planted will be at most three times the number of boxwoods planted, which can be represented by the inequality \(a \le 3b\). Therefore, the system of inequalities containing \(a + b \le 164\) and \(a\) \(\le 3b\) best represents this situation.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The inequality \(a+ b \ge 164\) represents a situation where at least 164 total bushes will be planted, not that there will be no more than 164 total bushes planted. Also, the inequality \(3a \ge b\) represents a situation where the number of boxwoods that will be planted is at most three times the number of azaleas that will be planted, not that the number of azaleas planted will be at most three times the number of boxwoods planted.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The inequality \(a+ b \ge 164\) represents a situation where at least 164 total bushes will be planted, not that there will be no more than 164 total bushes planted.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. The inequality \(3a \ge b\) represents a situation where the number of boxwoods that will be planted is at most three times the number of azaleas that will be planted, not that the number of azaleas planted will be at most three times the number of boxwoods planted.</p>' WHERE id='6daf8d70';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/4acd05cd_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>The graph shows the linear relationship between \(x\) and \(y\). Which table gives three values of \(x\) and their corresponding values of \(y\) for this relationship?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>-7</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>-9</td></tr></table></div>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>-1</td></tr></table></div>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>-5</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>-7</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>-9</td></tr></table></div>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>-5</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>-3</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>-1</td></tr></table></div>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It’s given that the graph shows the linear relationship between \(x\) and \(y\). The given graph passes through the points (0,-5) ,</p><ul><li>(1,-3) , and (2,-1) . It follows that when \(x = 0\), the corresponding value of \(y\) is -5, when \(x = 1\), the corresponding value of \(y\) is -3, and when \(x = 2\), the corresponding value of \(y\) is -1. Of the given choices, only the table in choice D gives these three values of \(x\) and their corresponding values of \(y\) for the relationship shown in the graph.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect. This table represents a relationship between \(x\) and \(y\) such that the graph passes through the points (0,0), (1,-7), and</p><ul><li>(2,-9) .</li></ul><p>Choice B is incorrect. This table represents a relationship between \(x\) and \(y\) such that the graph passes through the points (0,0) , (1,-3) , and</p><ul><li>(2,-1) .</li></ul><p>Choice C is incorrect. This table represents a linear relationship between \(x\) and \(y\) such that the graph passes through the points (0,-5) ,</p><ul><li>(1,-7) , and (2,-9) .</li></ul>' WHERE id='4acd05cd';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>For a particular car, the linear function \(f\) gives the predicted power, in brake horsepower \((bhp)\) , for engine speeds between</p><h3>Prompt</h3><ul><li>\(1,000\text{ revolutions per minute } (rpm)\) and \(6,000 rpm\). According to this function, the car''s predicted power is \(433 bhp\) at an engine speed of \(3,331 rpm\) and \(600 bhp\) at an engine speed of \(4,500 rpm\). The equation \(f(x)=\frac{1}{7}(x-a) +433\) defines \(f\), where \(x\) is the engine speed, in rpm, and \(a\) is a constant. What is the value of \(a\)?</li></ul>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["3331"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 3,331 . For engine speeds between \(1,000\text{ revolutions per minute } (rpm)\) and \(6,000 rpm\), it’s given that the equation \(f(x) =\frac{1}{7}(x-a)+433\) defines the linear function \(f\) and gives the predicted power, in brake horsepower \((bhp)\), at an engine speed of \(x rpm\), where \(a\) is a constant. It’s also given that the car''s predicted power is \(433 bhp\) at an engine speed of \(3,331 rpm\). Substituting 3,331 for \(x\) and 433 for \(f(x)\) in the equation yields \(433 =\frac{1}{7}(3,331-a)+433\). Subtracting 433 from both sides of this equation yields 0</p><ul><li>\(=\frac{1}{7}(3,331-a)\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by 7 yields \(0 = 3,331 - a\). Adding \(a\) to both sides of this equation yields \(a = 3,331\). Thus, the value of \(a\) is 3,331 .</li></ul>' WHERE id='63d03c0b';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>Argon is placed inside a container with a constant volume. The graph shows the estimated pressure \(y\), in pounds per square inch (psi), of the argon when its temperature is \(x\) kelvins.</p><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/d0cb49e8_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/d0cb49e8_m0.webp" alt=""> <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d0cb49e8_m1.webp" alt=""> <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d0cb49e8_m2.webp" alt=""> <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d0cb49e8_m3.webp" alt=""></p><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/d0cb49e8_m4.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>What is the estimated pressure of the argon, in \(psi\), when the temperature is 600 kelvins?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>6</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>12</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>300</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>600</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. For the graph shown, the x-axis represents temperature, in kelvins, and the y-axis represents the estimated pressure, in pounds per square inch (psi) . The estimated pressure of the argon when the temperature is 600 kelvins can be found by locating the point on the graph where the value of \(x\) is equal to 600. The graph passes through the point (600,12). This means that when the temperature is 600 kelvins, the estimated pressure is \(12 psi\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the estimated pressure, in \(psi\), of the argon when the temperature is 300 kelvins, not 600 kelvins.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the temperature, in kelvins, of the argon.</p>' WHERE id='d0cb49e8';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The length, \(y\), of a white whale was \(162\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) when it was born and increased an average of \(4.8 cm\) per month for the first 12 months after it was born. Which equation best represents this situation, where \(x\) is the number of months after the whale was born and \(y\) is the length, in \(cm\), of the whale?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(y = 162x\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(y = 162x + 162\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(y = 4.8x + 4.8\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(y = 4.8x + 162\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It''s given that the length of the whale was \(162 cm\) when it was born and that its length increased an average of \(4.8 cm\) per month for the first 12 months after it was born. Since \(x\) represents the number of months after the whale was born, the total increase in the whale''s length, in \(cm\), is 4.8 times \(x\), or \(4.8x\). The length of the whale \(y\), in \(cm\), can be found by adding the whale''s length at birth, \(162 cm\), to the total increase in length, \(4.8x cm\). Therefore, the equation that best represents this situation is \(y = 4.8x + 162\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>' WHERE id='cee5b352';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(11x + 14y \le 115\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>Anthony will spend at most $115 to purchase \(x\) small cheese pizzas and \(y\) large cheese pizzas for a team dinner. The given inequality represents this situation. Which of the following is the best interpretation of \(14y\) in this context?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>The amount, in dollars, Anthony will spend on each large cheese pizza</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>The amount, in dollars, Anthony will spend on each small cheese pizza</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>The total amount, in dollars, Anthony will spend on large cheese pizzas</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>The total amount, in dollars, Anthony will spend on small cheese pizzas</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It''s given that Anthony will spend at most $115 to purchase \(x\) small cheese pizzas and \(y\) large cheese pizzas. In the inequality \(11x + 14y \le 115\), \(y\) represents the number of large cheese pizzas that Anthony will purchase. This means the coefficient 14 represents the amount, in dollars, Anthony will spend on each large cheese pizza. Therefore, the best interpretation of \(14y\) in this context is the total amount, in dollars, Anthony will spend on large cheese pizzas.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the best interpretation of 14, not \(14y\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the best interpretation of 11, not \(14y\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the best interpretation of \(11x\), not \(14y\).</p>' WHERE id='68c5c81a';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>To repair a refrigerator, a technician charges $60 per hour for labor plus $120 for parts. Which function f represents the total amount, in dollars, the technician will charge for this job if it takes \(x\) hours?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(f(x) = x+ 120\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(f(x) = 60x\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(f(x) = 60x+ 120\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(f(x) = 60x- 120\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It’s given that the technician charges $60 per hour for labor. Therefore, if the job takes \(x\) hours, the technician will charge \((\frac{\$60}{1\text{ hour}})(x\text{ hours})\) , or \(\$60x\), for labor. It’s also given that the technician charges $120 for parts. Therefore, \(f(x) = 60x+ 120\) represents the total amount, in dollars, the technician will charge for this job if it takes \(x\) hours.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This function represents the total amount, in dollars, the technician charges for labor only, not the total amount charged for labor and parts.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This function represents the total amount, in dollars, the technician would charge if the charge for parts were subtracted from, rather than added to, the charge for labor.</p>' WHERE id='fe287f7e';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Figure A and figure B are both regular polygons. The sum of the perimeter of figure A and the perimeter of figure B is 63 inches. The equation \(3x + 6y = 63\) represents this situation, where \(x\) is the number of sides of figure A and \(y\) is the number of sides of figure B. Which statement is the best interpretation of 6 in this context?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>Each side of figure B has a length of 6 inches.</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>The number of sides of figure B is 6.</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>Each side of figure A has a length of 6 inches.</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>The number of sides of figure A is 6.</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that figure A and figure B (not shown) are both regular polygons and the sum of the perimeters of the two figures is \(63\text{ inches}\). It’s also given that \(x\) is the number of sides of figure A and \(y\) is the number of sides of figure B, and that the equation \(3x + 6y\) = 63 represents this situation. Thus, \(3x\) and \(6y\) represent the perimeters, in inches, of figure A and figure B, respectively. Since \(6y\) represents the perimeter, in inches, of figure B and \(y\) is the number of sides of figure B, it follows that each side of figure B has a length of \(6\text{ inches}\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The number of sides of figure B is \(y\), not 6.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. Since the perimeter, in inches, of figure A is represented by \(3x\), each side of figure A has a length of \(3\text{ inches}\), not \(6\text{ inches}\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. The number of sides of figure A is \(x\), not 6.</p>' WHERE id='2e1a7f66';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A rocket contained 467,000 kilograms (kg) of propellant before launch. Exactly 21 seconds after launch, 362,105 kg of this propellant remained. On average, approximately how much propellant, in kg, did the rocket burn each second after launch?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>4,995</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>17,243</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>39,481</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>104,895</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that the rocket contained \(467,000\text{ kilograms } (kg)\) of propellant before launch and had \(362,105 kg\) remaining exactly 21 seconds after launch. Finding the difference between the amount, in \(kg\), of propellant before launch and the remaining amount, in \(kg\), of propellant after launch gives the amount, in \(kg\), of propellant burned during the 21 seconds: 467,000- 362,105 = 104,895. Dividing the amount of propellant burned by the number of seconds yields \(\frac{104,895}{21}= 4,995\). Thus, an average of \(4,995 kg\) of propellant burned each second after launch.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from finding the amount of propellant burned, rather than the amount of propellant burned each second.</p>' WHERE id='fbb0ea7f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Sam has a flag with an area of \(110\text{ square inches } (\text{ in}^{2})\) . Sam plans to enlarge the flag by sewing on pieces of colored nylon, where each piece would increase the flag''s area by \(64\text{ in}^{2}\) . Which equation gives the flag''s total area \(y\), in square inches, after Sam sews on \(x\) pieces of nylon?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(y = 64x\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(y = 64 + 110x\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(y = 110 + 64x\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(y = 174x\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It''s given that Sam has a flag with an area of \(110\text{ square inches } (\text{ in}^{2})\) and that each piece of colored nylon Sam sews on increases the flag''s area by \(64\text{ in}^{2}\). If Sam sews on \(x\) pieces of nylon and each piece increases the area by \(64\text{ in}^{2}\) , it follows that the expression \(64x\) represents the total increase in area, in square inches, from sewing on \(x\) pieces of nylon. Adding this increase in area, \(64x\text{ in}^{2}\), to the original area of the flag, \(110\text{ in}^{2}\) , gives the total area of the flag. Therefore, the equation that gives the flag''s total area \(y\), in square inches, after Sam sews on \(x\) pieces of nylon is \(y = 110 + 64x\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This equation represents the total area, in square inches, of \(x\) pieces of colored nylon.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='0c490cd5';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(2ℓ+ 2w \le 27\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>A rectangle has length ℓ and width \(w\). The inequality gives the possible values of ℓ and \(w\) for which the perimeter of this rectangle is less than or equal to 27. Which statement is the best interpretation of \((ℓ,w) = (8,3)\) in this context?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>If the rectangle has length 3 and width 8, its perimeter is less than or equal to 27.</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>If the rectangle has length 8 and width 3, its perimeter is less than or equal to 27.</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>If the rectangle has length 3 and width 8, its perimeter is greater than or equal to 27.</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>If the rectangle has length 8 and width 3, its perimeter is greater than or equal to 27.</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It’s given that a rectangle has length ℓ and width \(w\), and the inequality \(2ℓ + 2w \le 27\) gives the possible values of ℓ and \(w\) for which the perimeter of this rectangle is less than or equal to 27. To determine the best interpretation of \((ℓ,w) = (8,3)\) in this context, the values can be substituted in the given inequality. Substituting 8 for ℓ and 3 for \(w\) in this inequality yields 2(8)+2(3) ≤ 27, which is equivalent to 16 + 6 ≤ 27, or 22 ≤ 27. Since this inequality is true, if the rectangle has length 8 and width 3, its perimeter is less than or equal to 27.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The interpretation of \((ℓ,w) = (8,3)\) implies that the rectangle has length 8 and width 3, not length 3 and width 8.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. The interpretation of \((ℓ,w) = (8,3)\) implies that the rectangle has length 8 and width 3, not length 3 and width 8.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='208626df';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(x + y = 53\)</p><p>\(11x + 18y = 730\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>The given equations represent the possible numbers of beach chairs, \(x\), and umbrellas, \(y\), rented at a park last month and the total spent, in dollars, to rent those beach chairs and umbrellas. Which of the following graphs represents this situation?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<div class=\"qfig\"><img src=\"/qimg/36f068e2_chA.webp\" alt=\"Choice A\"></div>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<div class=\"qfig\"><img src=\"/qimg/36f068e2_chB.webp\" alt=\"Choice B\"></div>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<div class=\"qfig\"><img src=\"/qimg/36f068e2_chC.webp\" alt=\"Choice C\"></div>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<div class=\"qfig\"><img src=\"/qimg/36f068e2_chD.webp\" alt=\"Choice D\"></div>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. The intercepts of the graph of each equation can be found by substituting 0 for one variable at a time. For the equation \(x\) \(+ y = 53\), substituting 0 for \(y\) yields \(x = 53\), so the x-intercept of the graph of the equation is (53,0) . Substituting 0 for \(x\) in this equation yields \(y = 53\), so the y-intercept of the graph of the equation is (0,53) . Thus, the graph of the equation \(x + y = 53\) has intercepts at (53,0) and (0,53). For the equation \(11x + 18y = 730\), substituting 0 for \(y\) yields \(x =\frac{730}{11}\) , so the x-intercept of the graph of the equation is approximately (66,0) . Substituting 0 for \(x\) in this equation yields \(y =\frac{730}{18}\) , so the y-intercept of the graph of the equation is approximately</p><ul><li>(0,41) . Thus, the graph of the equation \(11x + 18y = 730\) has intercepts at approximately (66,0) and (0,41) . Choice B is a graph that shows one line with intercepts at (0,53) and (53,0) and another line with intercepts at approximately (0,41) and (66,0) . Thus, choice B represents this situation.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect. This graph represents the equations \(y = x\) and \(11x + 18y = 730\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This graph represents the equations \(x + y = 53\) and \(-11x + 18y = 730\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This graph represents the equations \(y = 53\) and \(-11x + 18y = 730\).</p>' WHERE id='36f068e2';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Monarch butterflies can fly only with a body temperature of at least \(55.0\text{ degrees Fahrenheit } (^{\circ}F)\) . If a monarch butterfly''s body temperature is \(51.3^{\circ}F\), what is the minimum increase needed in its body temperature, in \(^{\circ}F\), so that it can fly?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>1.3</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>3.7</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>5.0</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>6.3</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that monarch butterflies can fly only with a body temperature of at least \(55.0\text{ degrees Fahrenheit } (^{\circ}F)\). Let \(x\) represent the minimum increase needed in the monarch butterfly''s body temperature to fly. If the monarch butterfly''s body temperature is 51.3</p><ul><li>\(^{\circ}F\), the inequality \(51.3 + x \ge 55.0\) represents this situation. Subtracting 51.3 from both sides of this inequality yields \(x \ge 3.7\). Therefore, if the monarch butterfly''s body temperature is \(51.3^{\circ}F\), the minimum increase needed in its body temperature, in \(^{\circ}F\), so that it can fly is 3.7.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the minimum increase needed in body temperature if the monarch butterfly''s body temperature is \(53.7^{\circ}F\), not 51.3</p><ul><li>\(^{\circ}F\).</li></ul><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the minimum increase needed in body temperature if the monarch butterfly''s body temperature is \(50.0^{\circ}F\), not 51.3</p><ul><li>\(^{\circ}F\).</li></ul><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the minimum increase needed in body temperature if the monarch butterfly''s body temperature is \(48.7^{\circ}F\), not 51.3</p><ul><li>\(^{\circ}F\).</li></ul>' WHERE id='b64e2c7f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An airplane descends from an altitude of 9,500 feet to 5,000 feet at a constant rate of 400 feet per minute. What type of function best models the relationship between the descending airplane''s altitude and time?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>Decreasing exponential</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>Decreasing linear</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>Increasing exponential</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>Increasing linear</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It′s given that the airplane descends at a constant rate of \(400\text{ feet per minute}\). Since the altitude decreases by a constant amount during each fixed time period, the relationship between the airplane′s altitude and time is linear. Since the airplane descends from an altitude of \(9,500\text{ feet}\) to \(5,000\text{ feet}\), the airplane′s altitude is decreasing with time. Thus, the relationship is best modeled by a decreasing linear function.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='43744269';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>To study the moisture content in a group of trees, samples from the trunk of each tree were taken from 25 trees and cut in the shape of a cube. The length of the edge of one of these cubes is 2.00 centimeters. If this cube has a mass of 2.56 grams, what is the density of this cube, in grams per cubic centimeter?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["0.32, 8/25"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is .32. The volume of a cube is given by the formula \(V = s^{3}\) , where \(s\) is the length of an edge of the cube. It’s given that each edge of the cube has a length of 2.00 centimeters. Substituting 2.00 centimeters for \(s\) in the formula \(V = s^{3}\) yields \(V\)</p><ul><li>\(= (2.00\text{ centimeters})^{3}\), or \(V = 8.00\) cubic centimeters. It’s given that the cube has a mass of 2.56 grams. Dividing the mass, in grams, of the cube by the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the cube gives its density, in grams per cubic centimeters. Therefore, the density of the cube is \(\frac{2.56\text{ grams}}{8.00\text{ cubic centimeters}}\) , or .32 grams per cubic centimeter. Note that .32 and 8/25 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</li></ul>' WHERE id='61f61789';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An area of 46.00 square nautical miles is equivalent to \(k\) square kilometers. To the nearest tenth, what is the value of \(k\)? \((1\text{ nautical mile} = 1.852\text{ kilometers})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["157.8, 789/5"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 157.8. It’s given that an area of 46.00 square nautical miles is equivalent to \(k\) square kilometers. Since 1 nautical mile is equal to 1.852 kilometers, it follows that an area of 1 square nautical mile is equivalent to \((1.852)^{2}\) , or 3.429904, square kilometers. Multiplying</p><ul><li>46.00 by 3.429904 yields 157.775584 square kilometers. Rounding this value to the nearest tenth yields 157.8. Therefore, the value of \(k\) is</li><li>157.8.</li></ul>' WHERE id='c74df824';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A rectangle has an area of 3,168 square inches. What is the area, in square feet, of this rectangle? \((1\text{ foot} = 12\text{ inches})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>22</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>56.3</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>264</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>675.4</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that \(1\text{ foot} = 12\text{ inches}\). It follows that \(1^{2}\text{ square foot} = 12^{2}\text{ square inches}\), or \(1\text{ square foot}\) \(= 144\text{ square inches}\). Thus, 3,168 square inches is equivalent to \((3,168\text{ square inches})(\frac{1\text{ square foot}}{144\text{ square inches}})\) , or 22 square feet.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{3,168}{12}\) , not \(\frac{3,168}{144}\) .</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='1b96c116';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>To study fluctuations in composition, samples of pumice were taken from 29 locations and cut in the shape of a cube. The length of the edge of one of these cubes is 3.000 centimeters. This cube has a density of 0.230 grams per cubic centimeter. What is the mass of this cube, in grams?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["6.21"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 6.21 . It’s given that the samples of pumice were cut in the shape of a cube. It''s also given that the length of the edge of one of these cubes is 3.000 centimeters. Therefore, the volume of this cube is \((3.000\text{ centimeters})^{3}\), or 27 cubic centimeters. Since the density of this cube is 0.230 grams per cubic centimeter, it follows that the mass of this cube is \((\frac{0.230\text{ grams}}{1\text{ cubic centimeter}})(27\text{ cubic centimeters})\) , or 6.21 grams.</p>' WHERE id='5154615f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many tablespoons are equivalent to 14 teaspoons? \((3\text{ teaspoons} = 1\text{ tablespoon})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["14/3, 4.666, 4.667"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{14}{3}\) . It''s given that 3 teaspoons is equivalent to 1 tablespoon. Therefore, 14 teaspoons is equivalent to</p><ul><li>\((14\text{ teaspoons})(\frac{1\text{ tablespoon}}{3\text{ teaspoons}})\) , or \(\frac{14}{3}\) tablespoons. Note that 14/3, 4.666, and 4.667 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</li></ul>' WHERE id='eb672707';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A certain bird species can fly at an average speed of 16 meters per second when in continuous flight. At this rate, how many meters would this bird species fly in 4 seconds?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>64</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>20</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>16</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>12</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It''s given that a certain bird species can fly at an average speed of 16 meters per second when in continuous flight. At this rate, in 4 seconds this bird species would fly \((\frac{16\text{ meters}}{\text{ second}})(4\text{ seconds})\) , or 64 meters.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of 16 + 4, not 16(4) .</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the distance the bird would fly in 1 second, not 4 seconds.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of 16 - 4, not 16(4).</p>' WHERE id='7270d642';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A special camera is used for underwater ocean research. The camera is at a depth of 39 fathoms. What is the camera''s depth in feet? \((1\text{ fathom} = 6\text{ feet})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>234</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>117</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>45</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>7</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that a special camera is used for underwater ocean research, and this camera is at a depth of 39 fathoms. It''s also given that 1 fathom is equal to 6 feet. Thus, 39 fathoms is equivalent to \((39\text{ fathoms})(\frac{6\text{ feet}}{1\text{ fathom}})\) , or 234 feet. Therefore, the camera''s depth, in feet, is 234.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the camera''s depth, in feet, if the camera is at a depth of 19.5 fathoms.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the camera''s depth, in feet, if the camera is at a depth of 7.5 fathoms.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='8bf3f67a';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A triathlon is a multisport race consisting of three different legs. A triathlon participant completed the cycling leg with an average speed of</p><ul><li>19.700 miles per hour. What was the average speed, in yards per hour, of the participant during the cycling leg? \((1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards})\)</li></ul>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["34672"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 34,672. It''s given that \(1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards}\). It follows that an average speed of 19.700 miles per hour is equivalent to</p><ul><li>\((\frac{19.700\text{ miles}}{1\text{ hour}})(\frac{1,760\text{ yards}}{1\text{ mile}})\) , or 34,672 yards per hour.</li></ul>' WHERE id='cb4894f9';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Objects R and S each travel at a constant speed. The speed of object R is half the speed of object S. Object R travels a distance of \(4x\) inches in \(y\) seconds. Which expression represents the time, in seconds, it takes object S to travel a distance of \(24x\) inches?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(12y\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(3y\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(16y\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(6y\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that object R travels a distance of \(4x\) inches in \(y\) seconds. This speed can be written as \(\frac{4x\text{ inches}}{y\text{ seconds}}\) . It''s given that the speed of object R is half the speed of object S. It follows that the speed of object S is twice the speed of object R, which is \(^{2}(\frac{4x\text{ inches}}{y\text{ seconds}})\) , or</p><ul><li>\(\frac{8x\text{ inches}}{y\text{ seconds}}\) . Let \(n\) represent the time, in seconds, it takes object S to travel a distance of \(24x\) inches. The value of \(n\) can be found by solving the equation \(\frac{8x\text{ inches}}{y\text{ seconds}}=\frac{24x\text{ inches}}{n\text{ seconds}}\) , which can be written as \(\frac{8x}{y}=\frac{24x}{n}\) . Multiplying each side of this equation by \(^{n}y\) yields \(8xn = 24xy\). Dividing each side of this equation by \(8x\) yields \(n = 3y\). Therefore, the expression \(3y\) represents the time, in seconds, it takes object S to travel a distance of \(24x\) inches.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect. This expression represents the time, in seconds, it would take object S to travel a distance of \(24x\) inches if the speed of object R were twice, not half, the speed of object S.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This expression represents the time, in seconds, it takes object S to travel a distance of \(128x\) inches, not \(24x\) inches.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This expression represents the time, in seconds, it takes object R, not object S, to travel a distance of \(24x\) inches.</p>' WHERE id='50b99b2d';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An object''s speed is \(64\text{ yards}\) per second. What is the object''s speed, in feet per second? \((1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>61</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>67</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>94</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>192</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. Since 1 yard is equal to 3 feet, 64 yards is equal to \(64\text{ yards }(\frac{3\text{ feet}}{1\text{ yard}})\) , or 192 feet. It follows that 64 yards per second is equivalent to 192 feet per second. Therefore, the object''s speed is 192 feet per second.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. A speed of 61 feet per second is equivalent to \(\frac{61}{3}\) , not 64, yards per second.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. A speed of 67 feet per second is equivalent to \(\frac{67}{3}\) , not 64, yards per second.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. A speed of 94 feet per second is equivalent to \(\frac{94}{3}\) , not 64, yards per second.</p>' WHERE id='c47256ca';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A distance of 354 furlongs is equivalent to how many feet? \((1\text{ furlong} = 220\text{ yards and } 1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>306</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>402</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>25,960</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>233,640</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It''s given that \(1\text{ furlong} = 220\text{ yards}\) and \(1\text{ yard} = 3\text{ feet}\). It follows that a distance of 354 furlongs is equivalent to</p><ul><li>\((354\text{ furlongs})(\frac{220\text{ yards}}{1\text{ furlong}})(\frac{3\text{ feet}}{1\text{ yard}})\) , or 233,640 feet.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='568bc2a4';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>One side of a flat board has an area of 874 square inches. If a pressure of 19 pounds per square inch of area is exerted on this side of the board, what is the total force, in pounds, exerted on this side of the board?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["16606"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 16,606. It''s given that one side of a flat board has an area of 874 square inches. If a pressure of 19 pounds per square inch of area is exerted on this side of the board, the total force exerted on this side of the board is \((874\text{ square inches})(\frac{19\text{ pounds}}{1\text{ square inch}})\) , or</p><ul><li>16,606 pounds.</li></ul>' WHERE id='96c3e32d';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A participant in a bicycle race completes the race with an average speed of 24,816 yards per hour. What is this average speed, in miles per hour? \((1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["14.1"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 14.1 . It’s given that a participant completes the bicycle race with an average speed of 24,816 yards per hour and \(1\text{ mile}\)</p><ul><li>\(= 1,760\text{ yards}\). It follows that this average speed is equivalent to \((\frac{24,816\text{ yards}}{1\text{ hour}})(\frac{1\text{ mile}}{1,760\text{ yards}})\) , which yields \(\frac{14.1\text{ miles}}{1\text{ hour}}\) , or 14.1 miles per hour.</li></ul>' WHERE id='94660ba8';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>For an electric field passing through a flat surface perpendicular to it, the electric flux of the electric field through the surface is the product of the electric field’s strength and the area of the surface. A certain flat surface consists of two adjacent squares, where the side length, in meters, of the larger square is 3 times the side length, in meters, of the smaller square. An electric field with strength 29.00 volts per meter passes uniformly through this surface, which is perpendicular to the electric field. If the total electric flux of the electric field through this surface is</p><h3>Prompt</h3><ul><li>\(4,640\text{ volts} .\text{ meters}\), what is the electric flux, in volts . meters, of the electric field through the larger square?</li></ul>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["4176"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 4,176. It’s given that the side length of the larger square is 3 times the side length of the smaller square. This means that the area of the larger square is \(3^{2}\), or 9, times the area of the smaller square. If the area of the smaller square is represented by \(x\), then the area of the larger square can be represented by \(9x\). Therefore, the flat surface of the two adjacent squares has a total area of \(x + 9x\), or \(10x\). It’s given that an electric field with strength 29.00 volts per meter passes uniformly through this surface and the total electric flux of the electric field through this surface is \(4,640\text{ volts} .\text{ meters}\). Since it''s given that the electric flux is the product of the electric field’s strength and the area of the surface, the equation \(29.00(10x)= 4,640\), or \(290x = 4,640\), can be used to represent this situation. Dividing each side of this equation by 290 yields \(x = 16\). Substituting 16 for \(x\) in the expression for the area of the larger square, \(9x\), yields 9(16), or 144, square meters. Since the area of the larger square is 144 square meters, the electric flux, in volts . meters, of the electric field through the larger square can be determined by multiplying the area of the larger square by the strength of the electric field. Thus, the electric flux is</p><ul><li>\((144\text{ square meters})(\frac{29.00\text{ volts}}{\text{ meter}})\) , or \(4,176\text{ volts} .\text{ meters}\).</li></ul>' WHERE id='c2e7fa6d';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An object travels at a constant speed of 6 centimeters per second. At this speed, what is the time, in seconds, that it would take for the object to travel 24 centimeters?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["4"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 4. It’s given that the object travels at a constant speed of 6 centimeters per second. The speed of the object can be written as \(\frac{6\text{ centimeters}}{1\text{ second}}\) . Let \(x\) represent the time, in seconds, it would take for the object to travel 24 centimeters. The value of \(x\) can be calculated by solving the equation <img class="minl" src="/qimg/4837406c_m0.webp" alt=""> , which can be written as \(\frac{6}{1}=\frac{24}{x}\) , or \(6 =\frac{24}{x}\) . Multiplying each side of this equation by \(x\) yields \(6x = 24\). Dividing each side of this equation by 6 yields \(x = 4\). Therefore, it would take the object 4 seconds to travel 24 centimeters.</p>' WHERE id='4837406c';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>The scatterplot shows the temperature \(y\), in \(^{\circ}F\), recorded by a meteorologist at various times \(x\), in days since June 1.</p><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/d112bc9d_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/d112bc9d_m0.webp" alt=""> <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d112bc9d_m1.webp" alt=""></p><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/d112bc9d_m2.webp" alt=""> </p><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/d112bc9d_m3.webp" alt=""> <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d112bc9d_m4.webp" alt=""></p><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/d112bc9d_m5.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>During which of the following time periods did the greatest increase in recorded temperature take place?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>From \\(x = 6\\) to \\(x = 7\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>From \\(x = 5\\) to \\(x = 6\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>From \\(x = 2\\) to \\(x = 3\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>From \\(x = 1\\) to \\(x = 2\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The scatterplot shows that there was an increase in recorded temperature from \(x = 2\) to \(x = 3\) and from \(x = 6\) to \(x = 7\). When \(x = 2\), the recorded temperature was approximately \(60^{\circ}F\) and when \(x = 3\), the recorded temperature was greater than \(70^{\circ}F\). This means that the increase in recorded temperature from \(x = 2\) to \(x = 3\) was greater than \((70-60)^{\circ}F\), or \(10^{\circ}F\). When \(x = 6\), the recorded temperature was greater than \(60^{\circ}F\) and when \(x = 7\), the recorded temperature was less than \(70^{\circ}F\). This means that the increase in recorded temperature from \(x = 6\) to \(x = 7\) was less than \((70-60)^{\circ}F\), or \(10^{\circ}F\). It follows that the greatest increase in recorded temperature took place from \(x = 2\) to \(x = 3\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The increase in recorded temperature from \(x = 6\) to \(x = 7\) was less than the increase in recorded temperature from \(x\) = 2 to \(x = 3\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. From \(x = 5\) to \(x = 6\), a decrease, not an increase, in recorded temperature took place.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. From \(x = 1\) to \(x = 2\), a decrease, not an increase, in recorded temperature took place.</p>' WHERE id='d112bc9d';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A product costs 11.00 dollars per pound. What is the cost, in dollars, for 6 pounds of the product?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["66"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 66. It’s given that a product costs 11.00 dollars per pound. Therefore, the cost for 6 pounds of the product is</p><ul><li>\((\frac{11.00\text{ dollars}}{1\text{ pound}})(6\text{ pounds})\) , which is equivalent to 66.00, or 66, dollars.</li></ul>' WHERE id='3318d37b';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What number is 40% greater than 115?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["161"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 161 . For a number to be 40% greater than 115, it follows that the number is \((100\%\text{ of } 115) + (40\%\text{ of } 115)\) , which can be written as \(\frac{100}{100}(115) +\frac{40}{100}(115)\) . This expression is equivalent to 1(115)+0.4(115) , or 1.4(115) , which is equal to 161 . Therefore, 161 is 40% greater than 115.</p>' WHERE id='7b731fc3';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A competition consisted of four different events. One participant completed the first event with an average speed of 20.300 miles per hour. What was this average speed, in yards per hour? \((1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["35728"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 35,728. It''s given that \(1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards}\). It follows that an average speed of 20.300 miles per hour is equivalent to</p><ul><li>\((\frac{20.300\text{ miles}}{1\text{ hour}})(\frac{1,760\text{ yards}}{1\text{ mile}})\) , or 35,728 yards per hour.</li></ul>' WHERE id='89c39d77';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/8193e8cd_m2.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>The mean of the list of numbers above is what fraction of the sum of the five numbers?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["1/5"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/8193e8cd_m0.webp" alt="">. The mean of the list of numbers is found by dividing the sum of the numbers by the number of values in the list. Since</p><p>there are 5 numbers in the list, the mean is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/8193e8cd_m1.webp" alt=""> of the sum of the numbers. Note that 1/5 and .2 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='8193e8cd';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The area of a rectangular region is increasing at a rate of 250 square feet per hour. Which of the following is closest to this rate in square meters per minute? <img class="minl" src="/qimg/7d093333_m0.webp" alt=""></p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0.39</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>1.27</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>13.67</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>23.24</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that \(1\text{ meter} = 3.28\text{ feet}\). It follows that \(1^{2}\text{ square meter} = 3.28^{2}\text{ square feet}\), or \(1\text{ square meter}\)</p><ul><li>\(= 10.7584\text{ square feet}\). Since \(1\text{ hour} = 60\text{ minutes}\), it follows that 250 square feet per hour is equivalent to</li><li>\((\frac{250\text{ square feet}}{1\text{ hour}})(\frac{1\text{ square meter}}{10.7584\text{ square feet}})(\frac{1\text{ hour}}{60\text{ minutes}})\) , or \(\frac{250\text{ square meters}}{645.504\text{ minutes}}\) , which is approximately 0.3873 square meters per minute. Of the given choices, 0.39 is closest to 0.3873.</li></ul><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='7d093333';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An insect moves at a speed of \(\frac{3}{20}\) feet per second. What is this speed, in yards per second? \((3\text{ feet} = 1\text{ yard})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{20}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{9}{20}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>6</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>20</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>3 Choice A is correct. It’s given that \(3\text{ feet} = 1\text{ yard}\). It follows that a speed of \(\frac{3}{20}\) feet per second is equivalent to <img class="minl" src="/qimg/674a4084_m0.webp" alt=""> , which is equivalent to \((\frac{3}{20})(\frac{1}{3})\) , or \(\frac{1}{20}\) , yards per second.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the speed, in feet per second, that''s equivalent to \(\frac{3}{20}\) yards per second.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the speed, in yards per second, that''s equivalent to 18, not \(\frac{3}{20}\) , feet per second.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the speed, in yards per second, that''s equivalent to 60, not \(\frac{3}{20}\) , feet per second.</p>' WHERE id='674a4084';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many cups are equivalent to 83 quarts? \((4\text{ cups} = 1\text{ quart})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>21</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>79</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>87</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>332</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It''s given that 4 cups is equal to 1 quart. Multiplying \(\frac{4\text{ cups}}{1\text{ quart}}\) by 83 quarts yields 4 . 83 cups, or 332 cups.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the number of quarts in 83 cups, rounded to the nearest whole number.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='b73025e2';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>The scatterplot shows the temperature, \(\text{ in degrees Fahrenheit } (^{\circ}F)\), and the distance above sea level, in feet, measured at 6 locations on Mount Jefferson. A line of best fit is also shown.</p><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/bc59c2d9_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><h3>Prompt</h3><p>At a distance of 4,000 feet above sea level, what is the temperature, \(\text{ in} ^{\circ}F\), predicted by the line of best fit?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>47</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>35</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>25</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>0</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. In the given scatterplot, the x-values represent the distance above sea level, in feet, and the y-values represent the temperature, in \(^{\circ}F\). The point on the line of best fit with an x-value of 4,000 has a corresponding y-value of 35. Therefore, at a distance of 4,000 feet above sea level, the temperature predicted by the line of best fit is \(35^{\circ}F\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the temperature, in \(^{\circ}F\), predicted by the line of best fit at a distance of 0 feet above sea level.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the measured temperature, in \(^{\circ}F\), at a distance of 6,000 feet above sea level.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='bc59c2d9';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many yards are equivalent to 1,116 inches? \((1\text{ yard} = 36\text{ inches})\)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["31"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 31 . It''s given that 1 yard is equal to 36 inches. Therefore, 1,116 inches is equivalent to <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d7a3179d_m0.webp" alt=""> , or 31 yards.</p>' WHERE id='d7a3179d';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A certain park has an area of 11,863,808 square yards. What is the area, in square miles, of this park? \((1\text{ mile} = 1,760\text{ yards})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>1.96</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>3.83</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>3,444.39</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>6,740.8</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. Since 1 mile is equal to 1,760 yards, 1 square mile is equal to \(1,760^{2}\), or 3,097,600, square yards. It’s given that the park has an area of 11,863,808 square yards. Therefore, the park has an area of \((11,863,808\text{ square yards})(\frac{1\text{ square mile}}{3,097,600\text{ square yards}})\) , or \(\frac{11,863,808}{3,097,600}\) square miles. Thus, the area, in square miles, of the park is 3.83.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the square root of the area of the park in square yards, not the area of the park in square miles.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from converting 11,863,808 yards to miles, rather than converting 11,863,808 square yards to square miles.</p>' WHERE id='d6456c7a';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many teaspoons are equivalent to 44 tablespoons? \((3\text{ teaspoons} = 1\text{ tablespoon})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>47</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>88</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>132</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>176</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It''s given that 3 teaspoons is equivalent to 1 tablespoon. Therefore, 44 tablespoons is equivalent to</p><ul><li>\((44\text{ tablespoons})(\frac{3\text{ teaspoons}}{1\text{ tablespoon}})\) , or 132 teaspoons.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is equivalent to approximately 15.66 tablespoons, not 44 tablespoons.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is equivalent to approximately 29.33 tablespoons, not 44 tablespoons.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is equivalent to approximately 58.66 tablespoons, not 44 tablespoons.</p>' WHERE id='4347a032';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A cluster fly is traveling at a velocity of 76 centimeters per second. What is this velocity, in millimeters per second? \((1\text{ centimeter} = 10\text{ millimeters})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>7,600</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>836</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>760</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>86</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It''s given that \(1\text{ centimeter} = 10\text{ millimeters}\). It follows that a velocity of 76 centimeters per second is equivalent to</p><ul><li>\((\frac{76\text{ centimeters}}{1\text{ second}}) (\frac{10\text{ millimeters}}{1\text{ centimeter}})\) , which is equivalent to (76) (10) , or 760, millimeters per second.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='de799680';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>How many meters are equivalent to 2,300 centimeters? \((100\text{ centimeters} = 1\text{ meter})\)</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0.043</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>23</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>2,400</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>230,000</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that 100 centimeters is equal to 1 meter. Therefore, 2,300 centimeters is equivalent to</p><ul><li>\((2,300\text{ centimeters})(\frac{1\text{ meter}}{100\text{ centimeters}})\) , or 23 meters.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect. 0.043 meters is equivalent to 4.3, not 2,300, centimeters.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. 2,400 meters is equivalent to 240,000, not 2,300, centimeters.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. 230,000 meters is equivalent to 23,000,000, not 2,300, centimeters.</p>' WHERE id='4e375d1f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Triangle \(FGH\) is similar to triangle \(JKL\), where angle \(F\) corresponds to angle \(J\) and angles \(G\) and \(K\) are right angles. If \(\sin (F) =\frac{308}{317}\) , what is the value of \(\sin (J)\) ?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{75}{317}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{308}{317}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{317}{308}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{317}{75}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. If two triangles are similar, then their corresponding angles are congruent. It''s given that right triangle \(FGH\) is similar to right triangle \(JKL\) and angle \(F\) corresponds to angle \(J\). It follows that angle \(F\) is congruent to angle \(J\) and, therefore, the measure of angle \(F\) is equal to the measure of angle \(J\). The sine ratios of angles of equal measure are equal. Since the measure of angle \(F\) is equal to the measure of angle \(J\), \(\sin (F) = \sin (J)\) . It''s given that \(\sin (F) =\frac{308}{317}\) . Therefore, \(\sin (J)\) is \(\frac{308}{317}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the value of \(\cos (J)\) , not the value of \(\sin (J)\) .</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the reciprocal of the value of \(\sin (J)\), not the value of \(\sin (J)\) .</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the reciprocal of the value of \(\cos (J)\), not the value of \(\sin (J)\).</p>' WHERE id='bcb66188';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In right triangle \(RST\), the sum of the measures of angle \(R\) and angle \(S\) is 90 degrees. The value of \(\sin (R)\) is \(\frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\) . What is the value of \(\cos (S)\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\sqrt{15}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. The sine of any acute angle is equal to the cosine of its complement. It’s given that in right triangle \(RST\), the sum of the measures of angle \(R\) and angle \(S\) is 90 degrees. Therefore, angle \(R\) and angle \(S\) are complementary, and the value of \(\sin R\) is equal to the value of \(\cos S\). It''s given that the value of \(\sin R\) is \(\frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\) , so the value of \(\cos S\) is also \(\frac{\sqrt{15}}{4}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the value of \(\tan S\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{1}{\cos S}\) .</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{1}{\tan S}\) .</p>' WHERE id='33e29881';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The side length of a square is \(55\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) . What is the area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\), of the square?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>110</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>220</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>3,025</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>12,100</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The area \(A\), \(\text{ in square centimeters } (cm^{2})\), of a square with side length \(s\), in cm, is given by the formula \(A = s^{2}\). It’s given that the square has a side length of \(55 cm\). Substituting 55 for \(s\) in the formula \(A = s^{2}\) yields \(A = 55^{2}\), or \(A = 3,025\). Therefore, the area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\), of the square is 3,025.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the perimeter, in cm, of the square, not its area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='5252e606';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Triangle R has an area of \(80\text{ square centimeters } (cm^{2})\) . Square S has side lengths of \(4 cm\). What is the total area of triangle R and square S, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>42</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>44</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>84</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>96</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It’s given that triangle R has an area of \(80 cm^{2}\). The area of a square is \(ℓ^{2}\), where ℓ is the side length of the square. It''s given that square S has side lengths of \(4 cm\). It follows that the area, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/bb560789_m0.webp" alt=""> , of square S is \(4^{2}\), or 16. Therefore, the total area, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/bb560789_m1.webp" alt="">, of triangle R and square S is 80 + 16, or 96.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='bb560789';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In triangle \(XYZ\), angle \(Z\) is a right angle and the length of \(YZ\) is 24 units. If \(\tan X =\frac{12}{35}\) , what is the perimeter, in units, of triangle \(XYZ\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>188</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>168</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>84</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>71</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that angle \(Z\) in triangle \(XYZ\) is a right angle. Thus, side \(YZ\) is the leg opposite angle \(X\) and side \(XZ\) is the leg adjacent to angle \(X\). The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that \(\tan X =\frac{YZ}{XZ}\) . It''s given that \(\tan X =\frac{12}{35}\) and the length of side \(YZ\) is 24 units. Substituting \(\frac{12}{35}\) for \(\tan X\) and 24 for \(YZ\) in the equation \(\tan X =\frac{YZ}{XZ}\) yields \(\frac{12}{35}=\frac{24}{XZ}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by \(35(XZ)\) yields \(12(XZ)\) = 24(35) , or \(12(XZ) = 840\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 12 yields \(XZ = 70\). The length \(XY\) can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem, which states that if a right triangle has legs with lengths of \(a\) and \(b\) and a hypotenuse with length \(c\), then \(a^{2}+ b^{2}= c^{2}\). Substituting 70 for \(a\) and 24 for \(b\) in this equation yields \(70^{2}+ 24^{2}= c^{2}\) , or \(5,476 = c^{2}\). Taking the square root of both sides of this equation yields \(\pm 74 = c\). Since the length of the hypotenuse must be positive, \(74 = c\). Therefore, the length of \(XY\) is 74 units. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of all sides. Thus, (74+70+24) units, or 168 units, is the perimeter of triangle \(XYZ\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This would be the perimeter, in units, for a right triangle where the length of side \(YZ\) is 12 units, not 24 units.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='a4c0547f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/659cb706_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>Triangle \(XYZ\) shown is a right triangle. Which of the following has the same value as \(\sin X\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<p>\\(\\tan X\\)</p>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<p>\\(\\tan Y\\)</p>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<p>\\(\\cos X\\)</p>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<p>\\(\\cos Y\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. The sine of an angle is equal to the cosine of its complementary angle. In the triangle shown, angle \(Z\) is a right angle; thus, angles \(X\) and \(Y\) are complementary angles. Therefore, \(\cos Y\) has the same value as \(\sin X\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>' WHERE id='659cb706';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>For two acute angles, \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\), \(\cos (Q) = \sin (R)\) . The measures, in degrees, of \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\) are \(x + 52\) and \(4x + 13\), respectively. What is the value of \(x\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>5</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>13</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>23</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>38</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It’s given that for two acute angles, \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\), \(\cos (Q) = \sin (R)\) . For two acute angles, if the sine of one angle is equal to the cosine of the other, the angles are complementary. Therefore, the sum of the measures, in degrees, of \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\) is 90. It’s given that the measures, in degrees, of \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\) are \(x + 52\) and \(4x + 13\), respectively. Therefore, \((x+52) + (4x+ 13) = 90\). Combining like terms in this equation yields \(5x + 65 = 90\). Subtracting 65 from each side of this equation results in \(5x = 25\). Dividing each side of this equation by 5 yields \(x = 5\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This would be the value of \(x\) if \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\) were congruent rather than complementary.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='27d075d8';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Two nearby trees are perpendicular to the ground, which is flat. One of these trees is 10 feet tall and has a shadow that is 5 feet long. At the same time, the shadow of the other tree is 2 feet long. How tall, in feet, is the other tree?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>3</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>4</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>8</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>27</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. Each tree and its shadow can be modeled using a right triangle, where the height of the tree and the length of its shadow are the legs of the triangle. At a given point in time, the right triangles formed by two nearby trees and their respective shadows will be similar. Therefore, if the height of the other tree is \(x\), in feet, the value of \(x\) can be calculated by solving the proportional relationship \(\frac{10\text{ feet tall}}{5\text{ feet long}}\)</p><ul><li>\(=\frac{x\text{ feet tall}}{2\text{ feet long}}\) . This equation is equivalent to \(\frac{10}{5}=\frac{x}{2}\) , or \(2 =\frac{x}{2}\) . Multiplying each side of the equation \(2 =\frac{x}{2}\) by 2 yields \(4 = x\). Therefore, the other tree is \(4\text{ feet}\) tall.</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from calculating the difference between the lengths of the shadows, rather than the height of the other tree.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from calculating the difference between the height of the 10-foot-tall tree and the length of the shadow of the other tree, rather than calculating the height of the other tree.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from a conceptual or calculation error.</p>' WHERE id='94364a79';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/2bddbc1b_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>What is the value of \(\cos A\) in the triangle shown?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{42}{41}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{41}{42}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{42}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{41}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. The cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the leg adjacent to the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. In the triangle shown, the length of the leg adjacent to angle \(A\) is 41, and the length of the hypotenuse is 42. Therefore, \(\cos A =\frac{41}{42}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{1}{\cos A}\) .</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='2bddbc1b';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In triangle \(XYZ\), angle \(Y\) is a right angle, the measure of angle \(Z\) is 33°, and the length of \(YZ\) is 26 units. If the area, in square units, of triangle \(XYZ\) can be represented by the expression \(ktan33^{\circ}\), where \(k\) is a constant, what is the value of \(k\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["338"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 338. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. In triangle \(XYZ\), it''s given that angle \(Y\) is a right angle. Thus, \(XY\) is the leg opposite of angle \(Z\) and \(YZ\) is the leg adjacent to angle \(Z\). It follows that \(\tan Z =\frac{XY}{YZ}\) . It''s also given that the measure of angle \(Z\) is 33° and the length of \(YZ\) is 26 units. Substituting 33° for \(Z\) and 26 for \(YZ\) in the equation \(\tan Z =\frac{XY}{YZ}\) yields \(\tan 33^{\circ}=\frac{XY}{26}\) . Multiplying each side of this equation by 26 yields</p><ul><li>\(26\tan 33^{\circ}= XY\). Therefore, the length of \(XY\) is \(26\tan 33^{\circ}\). The area of a triangle is half the product of the lengths of its legs. Since the length of \(YZ\) is 26 and the length of \(XY\) is \(26\tan 33^{\circ}\), it follows that the area of triangle \(XYZ\) is \(\frac{1}{2}(26)(26\tan 33^{\circ})\) square units, or</li><li>\(338\tan 33^{\circ}\) square units. It''s given that the area, in square units, of triangle \(XYZ\) can be represented by the expression \(ktan33^{\circ}\), where \(k\) is a constant. Therefore, 338 is the value of \(k\).</li></ul>' WHERE id='85f1892d';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/87a9a2d4_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In the right triangle shown, what is the value of \(\sin A\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{171}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{35}{171}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{171}{35}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>171</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. The sine of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the side opposite that angle to the length of the hypotenuse. The hypotenuse of a right triangle is the side opposite the right angle. In right triangle \(ABC\), side \(BC\) is the side opposite angle \(A\) and side \(AB\) is the hypotenuse. It''s given that the length of side \(BC\) is 35 units and the length of side \(AB\) is 171 units. Therefore, the value of \(\sin A\) is \(\frac{35}{171}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the ratio of the length of the hypotenuse to the length of the side opposite angle \(A\) rather than the ratio of the length of the side opposite angle \(A\) to the length of the hypotenuse.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the length of the hypotenuse rather than \(\sin A\).</p>' WHERE id='87a9a2d4';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/52f7b898_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>Right triangle \(ABC\) is shown. What is the value of \(\tan A\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{54}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\sqrt{3}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(27\\sqrt{3}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. In the triangle shown, the measure of angle \(B\) is 30° and angle \(C\) is a right angle, which means that it has a measure of 90°. Since the sum of the angles in a triangle is equal to 180°, the measure of angle \(A\) is equal to 180°-(30+90)°, or 60°. In a right triangle whose acute angles have measures 30° and 60°, the lengths of the legs can be represented by the expressions \(x\), \(x\sqrt{3}\), and \(2x\), where \(x\) is the length of the leg opposite the angle with measure 30°, \(x\sqrt{3}\) is the length of the leg opposite the angle with measure 60°, and \(2x\) is the length of the hypotenuse. In the triangle shown, the hypotenuse has a length of 54. It follows that \(2x = 54\), or \(x = 27\). Therefore, the length of the leg opposite angle \(B\) is 27 and the length of the leg opposite angle \(A\) is \(27\sqrt{3}\). The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. The length of the leg opposite angle \(A\) is \(27\sqrt{3}\) and the length of the leg adjacent to angle \(A\) is 27. Therefore, the value of \(\tan A\) is \(\frac{27\sqrt{3}}{27}\) , or \(\sqrt{3}\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{1}{\tan A}\) , not the value of \(\tan A\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the length of the leg opposite angle \(A\), not the value of \(\tan A\).</p>' WHERE id='52f7b898';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>Right rectangular prism X is similar to right rectangular prism Y. The surface area of right rectangular prism X is \(58\text{ square centimeters } (cm^{2})\) , and the surface area of right rectangular prism Y is \(1,450 cm^{2}\) . The volume of right rectangular prism Y is</p><h3>Prompt</h3><ul><li>\(1,250\text{ cubic centimeters } (cm^{3})\) . What is the sum of the volumes, \(\text{ in } cm^{3}\) , of right rectangular prism X and right rectangular prism Y?</li></ul>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["1260"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 1,260. Since it''s given that prisms X and Y are similar, all the linear measurements of prism Y are \(k\) times the respective linear measurements of prism X, where \(k\) is a positive constant. Therefore, the surface area of prism Y is \(k^{2}\) times the surface area of prism X and the volume of prism Y is \(k^{3}\) times the volume of prism X. It''s given that the surface area of prism Y is \(1,450 cm^{2}\) , and the surface area of prism X is \(58 cm^{2}\), which implies that \(1,450 = 58k^{2}\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 58 yields \(\frac{1,450}{58}= k^{2}\), or \(k^{2}= 25\). Since \(k\) is a positive constant, \(k = 5\). It''s given that the volume of prism Y is \(1,250 cm^{3}\). Therefore, the volume of prism X is equal to <img class="minl" src="/qimg/167aff9e_m0.webp" alt="">, which is equivalent to <img class="minl" src="/qimg/167aff9e_m1.webp" alt=""> , or \(10 cm^{3}\). Thus, the sum of the volumes, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/167aff9e_m2.webp" alt=""> , of the two prisms is 1,250 + 10, or 1,260.</p>' WHERE id='167aff9e';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/1429dcdf_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In the triangle shown, what is the value of \(\sin x^{\circ}\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='[".6956, .6957, 16/23"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{16}{23}\) . In a right triangle, the sine of an acute angle is defined as the ratio of the length of the side opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. In the triangle shown, the length of the side opposite the angle with measure \(x^{\circ}\) is 16 units and the length of the hypotenuse is 23 units. Therefore, the value of \(\sin x^{\circ}\) is \(\frac{16}{23}\) . Note that 16/23, .6956, .6957, 0.695, and 0.696 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='1429dcdf';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In \(\triangle ABC\), \(∠B\) is a right angle and the length of \(BC\) is 136 millimeters. If \(\cos A =\frac{3}{5}\) , what is the length, in millimeters, of \(AB\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>34</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>102</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>136</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>170</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that in \(\triangle ABC\), \(∠B\) is a right angle. Therefore, \(\triangle ABC\) is a right triangle, and \(AC\) is the hypotenuse of the triangle. It''s also given that \(\cos A =\frac{3}{5}\) . Since the cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the side adjacent to the angle to the length of the hypotenuse, the ratio of the length of \(AB\) to the length of \(AC\) is 3 to 5. It follows that the length of \(AB\) can be represented as \(3a\) and the length of \(AC\) can be represented as \(5a\), where \(a\) is a constant. The Pythagorean theorem states that the sum of the squares of the length of the legs of a right triangle is equal to the square of the length of its hypotenuse, so it follows that \(AB^{2}+ BC^{2}\) \(= AC^{2}\). Substituting \(3a\) for \(AB\) and \(5a\) for \(AC\) in this equation yields \((3a)^{2}+BC^{2}= (5a)^{2}\) , or \(9a^{2}+ BC^{2}= 25a^{2}\). Subtracting \(9a^{2}\) from both sides of this equation yields \(BC^{2}= 16a^{2}\) , or \(BC = 4a\). It follows that the ratio of the length of \(AB\) to the length of \(BC\) is 3 to 4. Let \(x\) represent the length, in millimeters, of \(AB\). It''s given that the length of \(BC\) is 136 millimeters. Since the ratio of the length of \(AB\) to the length of \(BC\) is 3 to 4, \(\frac{x}{136}=\frac{3}{4}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by 136 yields \(x =\frac{3(136)}{4}\) , or \(x = 102\). Therefore, the length of \(AB\) is 102 millimeters.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the scale factor by which the 3 to 4 to 5 ratio is multiplied that results in the side lengths of \(\triangle ABC\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the length of \(BC\), not the length of \(AB\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the length of \(AC\), not the length of \(AB\).</p>' WHERE id='5a7e3b46';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A right circular cylinder has a height of \(8\text{ meters } (m)\) and a base with a radius of \(12 m\). What is the volume, \(\text{ in } m^{3}\), of the cylinder?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>8\u03c0</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>20\u03c0</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>768\u03c0</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>1,152\u03c0</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. The volume, \(V\), of a right circular cylinder is given by \(V = \pi r^{2}h\), where \(r\) is the radius of the circular base and \(h\) is the height of the cylinder. It’s given that the cylinder has a height of 8 meters and a base with a radius of 12 meters. Substituting 12 for \(r\) and 8 for \(h\) in \(V\) \(= \pi r^{2}h\) yields \(V= \pi (12)^{2}(8)\) , or \(V = 1,152\pi \). Therefore, the volume, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a2659088_m0.webp" alt=""> , of the cylinder is 1,152π.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the volume, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a2659088_m1.webp" alt="">, of a cylinder with a radius of 8 meters and a height of 12 meters.</p>' WHERE id='a2659088';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/2757549e_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/2757549e_m0.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>In the figure, \(BC\) is parallel to \(DE\). If the length of \(DE\) is 147 and the length of \(AE\) is 245, what is the value of \(\tan x^{\circ}\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{147}{196}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{147}{245}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{196}{147}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{245}{147}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. It’s given that the length of \(DE\) is 147, and the length of \(AE\) is 245. Since angle \(D\) is a right angle, triangle \(ADE\) is a right triangle with hypotenuse \(AE\) and legs \(AD\) and \(DE\). By the Pythagorean theorem, if a right triangle has a hypotenuse with length \(c\) and legs with lengths \(a\) and \(b\), then \(a^{2}+ b^{2}= c^{2}\) . Substituting \(AD\) for \(a\), 147 for \(b\), and 245 for \(c\) in the equation \(a^{2}+ b^{2}= c^{2}\) yields \(AD^{2}+ 147^{2}= 245^{2}\). Subtracting \(147^{2}\) from both sides of this equation yields \(AD^{2}= 245^{2}- 147^{2}\) . Evaluating this difference yields \(AD^{2}= 60,025 - 21,609\), or</p><ul><li>\(AD^{2}= 38,416\). Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields \(AD = 196\). It''s given that \(BC\) is parallel to \(DE\), and \(AE\) intersects \(BC\) and \(DE\). It follows that angle \(ACB\) is congruent to angle \(AED\). Since angle \(A\) is common to triangles \(ABC\) and \(ADE\), it follows that triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(ADE\). In triangle \(ABC\), \(\tan x^{\circ}\) is the ratio of the leg opposite angle \(ACB\) to the leg adjacent to angle \(ACB\), so \(\tan x^{\circ}=\frac{AB}{BC}\) . Since triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(ADE\) and similar triangles have proportional side lengths, it follows that \(\frac{AB}{BC}=\frac{AD}{DE}\) . Therefore, \(\tan x^{\circ}=\frac{AD}{DE}\) . Substituting 196 for \(AD\) and 147 for \(DE\) in this equation yields \(\tan x^{\circ}=\frac{196}{147}\) . Therefore, the value of \(\tan x^{\circ}\) is \(\frac{196}{147}\) .</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of \(\cos x^{\circ}\), not \(\tan x^{\circ}\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='2757549e';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/c4a9c94d_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/c4a9c94d_m0.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>In the figure, \(AD\) and \(BC\) intersect at point \(D\). If the length of \(BD\) is 5 and the tangent of angle \(CAD\) is 1.3, what is the length of \(AD\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["6.5, 13/2"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 6.5. The figure shows that \(\triangle BAC\) is a right triangle. In the figure, \(AD\) is perpendicular to \(BC\), which forms two additional right triangles: \(\triangle BDA\) and \(\triangle ADC\). If two pairs of corresponding angles in two triangles are congruent, then the triangles are similar. \(\triangle BDA\) shares angle \(B\) with \(\triangle BAC\) and \(\triangle ADC\) shares angle \(C\) with \(\triangle BAC\). Therefore, the figure contains three similar right triangles: \(\triangle BAC\), \(\triangle BDA\), and \(\triangle ADC\). In similar triangles, the tangents of corresponding angles are equivalent. It''s given that the tangent of angle \(CAD\) is 1.3. Since angle \(CAD\) in \(\triangle ADC\) corresponds to angle \(ABD\) in \(\triangle BDA\), it follows that the tangent of angle \(ABD\) is also 1.3. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is equal to the length of the leg opposite the angle divided by the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. Therefore, \(\tan ABD =\frac{AD}{BD}\) . It''s given that the length of \(BD\) is 5. Substituting 5 for \(BD\) and 1.3 for \(\tan ABD\) in the equation \(\tan ABD =\frac{AD}{BD}\) yields \(1.3 =\frac{AD}{5}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by 5 yields \(6.5 = AD\). Therefore, the length of \(AD\) is 6.5. Note that 6.5 and 13/2 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='c4a9c94d';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/64c1f044_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In the triangle shown, what is the value of \(\tan x^{\circ}\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{26}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{19}{26}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{26}{7}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{33}{7}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the side opposite the angle to the length of the shorter side adjacent to the angle. In the triangle shown, the length of the side opposite the angle with measure \(x^{\circ}\) is 26 units and the length of the side adjacent to the angle with measure \(x^{\circ}\) is 7 units. Therefore, the value of \(\tan x^{\circ}\) is \(\frac{26}{7}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='64c1f044';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Acute angle \(P\) has a measure of \(p\) radians, and acute angle \(T\) has a measure of \(t\) radians. The function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x) =p(8)^{x}+6t\). If \(\sin p = \cos t\), which of the following represents the value \(g(0)\) in terms of \(t\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(6t + \\pi \\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(6t\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(5t + \\pi \\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(5t +\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It’s given that acute angle \(P\) has a measure of \(p\) radians and that acute angle \(T\) has a measure of \(t\) radians. If \(\sin p = \cos t\), it follows that \(p+ t =\frac{\pi }{2}\) , or \(p =\frac{\pi }{2}- t\). It’s also given that function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x) =p(8)^{x}+6t\). To determine the value of \(g(0)\) in terms of \(t\), substituting 0 for \(x\) and \(\frac{\pi }{2}- t\) for \(p\) in the equation \(g(x) =p(8)^{x}+6t\) yields \(g(0)= (\frac{\pi }{2}-t)(8)^{0}+6t\), or <img class="minl" src="/qimg/e3158182_m0.webp" alt=""> \(+ 6t\). This is equivalent to \(g(0)=\frac{\pi }{2}- t + 6t\), or \(g(0) = 5t+\frac{\pi }{2}\) . Therefore, \(5t +\frac{\pi }{2}\) represents the value of \(g(0)\) in terms of \(t\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='e3158182';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/94f7c9e2_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In triangle \(QRS\) shown, \(QR&lt; RS\). Which expression represents the length of \(QS\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<p>\\(18\\cos Q\\)</p>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<p>\\(18\\sin Q\\)</p>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<p>\\(\\frac{18}{\\cos Q}\\)</p>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<p>\\(\\frac{18}{\\sin Q}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. The figure shows that triangle \(QRS\) is a right triangle. Each of the given choices is an expression containing \(\sin Q\) or \(\cos Q\). For an acute angle in a right triangle, the sine of the angle is the length of the opposite leg divided by the length of the hypotenuse, and the cosine of the angle is the length of the adjacent leg divided by the length of the hypotenuse. It follows that \(\sin Q =\frac{RS}{QS}\) and <img class="minl" src="/qimg/94f7c9e2_m0.webp" alt=""> , where \(QR\) \(&lt; RS\). It''s given in the figure that the length of side \(RS\) is 18. Since only the equation \(\sin Q=\frac{RS}{QS}\) contains \(RS\), an expression representing \(QS\) can be found by substituting 18 for \(RS\) in this equation, which yields \(\sin Q =\frac{18}{QS}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by \(QS\) yields \(QS\)</p><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/94f7c9e2_fig1.webp" alt="Figure"></div><ul><li>\(. \sin Q = 18\). Dividing both sides of this equation by \(\sin Q\) yields \(QS=\frac{18}{\sin Q}\) . Therefore, the expression \(\frac{18}{\sin Q}\) represents the length of \(QS\).</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='94f7c9e2';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A right circular cone has a height of \(22\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) and a base with a diameter of \(6 cm\). The volume of this cone is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/899c6042_m0.webp" alt=""> . What is the value of \(n\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["66"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 66. It’s given that the right circular cone has a height of 22 centimeters \((cm)\) and a base with a diameter of \(6 cm\). Since the diameter of the base of the cone is \(6 cm\), the radius of the base is \(3 cm\). The volume \(V\), \(\text{ in } cm^{3}\), of a right circular cone can be found using the formula \(V =\frac{1}{3}\pi r^{2}h\), where \(h\) is the height, in cm, and \(r\) is the radius, in cm, of the base of the cone. Substituting 22 for \(h\) and 3 for \(r\) in this formula yields \(V =\frac{1}{3}\pi (3)^{2}(22)\) , or \(V = 66\pi \). Therefore, the volume of the cone is \(66\pi cm^{3}\). It’s given that the volume of the cone is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/899c6042_m1.webp" alt="">. Therefore, the value of \(n\) is 66.</p>' WHERE id='899c6042';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Circle \(K\) has a radius of \(4\text{ millimeters } (mm)\) . Circle \(L\) has an area of \(100\pi mm^{2}\) . What is the total area, \(\text{ in } mm^{2}\), of circles \(K\) and \(L\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>14\u03c0</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>28\u03c0</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>56\u03c0</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>116\u03c0</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. The area, \(A\), of a circle is given by the formula \(A = \pi r^{2}\) , where \(r\) represents the radius of the circle. It’s given that circle \(K\) has a radius of \(4\text{ millimeters } (mm)\) . Substituting 4 for \(r\) in the formula \(A = \pi r^{2}\) yields \(A= \pi (4)^{2}\) , or \(A = 16\pi \). Therefore, the area of circle \(K\) is \(16\pi mm^{2}\). It’s given that circle \(L\) has an area of \(100\pi mm^{2}\). Therefore, the total area, \(\text{ in } mm^{2}\), of circles \(K\) and \(L\) is 16π+ 100π, or 116π.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the sum of the radii, in mm, of circles \(K\) and \(L\) multiplied by π, not the total area, \(\text{ in } mm^{2}\), of the circles.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of the diameters, in mm, of circles \(K\) and \(L\) multiplied by π, not the total area, \(\text{ in } mm^{2}\), of the circles.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='98c12e38';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In triangle \(JKL\), the measures of \(∠K\) and \(∠L\) are each 48°. What is the measure of \(∠J\), in degrees? (Disregard the degree symbol when entering your answer.)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["84"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 84. The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is 180°. It''s given that in triangle \(JKL\), the measures of \(∠K\) and \(∠L\) are each 48°. Adding the measures, in degrees, of \(∠K\) and \(∠L\) gives 48 + 48, or 96. Therefore, the measure of \(∠J\), in degrees, is 180 - 96, or 84.</p>' WHERE id='fd8745fc';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A right circular cone has a height of \(22\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) and a base with a diameter of \(18 cm\). The volume of this cone is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/179edb12_m0.webp" alt="">. What is the value of \(n\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["594"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 594. It’s given that the right circular cone has a height of \(22 cm\) and a base with a diameter of \(18 cm\). Since the diameter of the base of the cone is \(18 cm\), the radius of the base is \(9 cm\). The volume \(V\), in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/179edb12_m1.webp" alt="">, of a right circular cone can be found using the formula \(V =\frac{1}{3}\pi r^{2}h\), where \(h\) is the height, in \(cm\), of the cone and \(r\) is the radius, in \(cm\), of the base of the cone. Substituting 22 for \(h\) and 9 for \(r\) in this formula yields \(V =\frac{1}{3}\pi (9)^{2}(22)\) , or \(V = 594\pi \). Therefore, the volume of the cone is \(594\pi cm^{3}\) . It’s given that the volume of the cone is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/179edb12_m2.webp" alt="">. Therefore, the value of \(n\) is 594.</p>' WHERE id='179edb12';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/b5d62bba_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/b5d62bba_m2.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>In the figure, \(LQ\) intersects \(MP\) at point \(R\), and \(LM\) is parallel to \(PQ\). The lengths of \(MR\) and \(RP\) are 8 and 14 units, respectively. The area of \(\triangle LMR\) is 36 square units. What is the area of \(\triangle PQR\), in square units?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{576}{49}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{144}{7}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>63</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{441}{4}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. The figure shows that \(∠MRL\) and \(∠PRQ\) are vertical angles. Since vertical angles are congruent, \(∠MRL\) and \(∠PRQ\) are congruent. It’s given that \(LM\) is parallel to \(PQ\). The figure also shows that \(LQ\) intersects \(LM\) and \(PQ\). If two parallel segments are intersected by a third segment, alternate interior angles are congruent. Thus, \(∠MLR\) and \(∠PQR\) are congruent. Since \(\triangle LMR\) and \(\triangle PQR\) have two pairs of congruent angles, the triangles are similar, where \(MR\) in \(\triangle LMR\) corresponds to \(PR\) in \(\triangle PQR\). Since the areas of similar triangles are proportional to the squares of the lengths of corresponding sides, it follows that \(\frac{\text{ area of } \triangle PQR}{\text{ area of } \triangle LMR}= (\frac{RP}{MR})^{2}\) . It’s given that the lengths of \(MR\) and \(RP\) are 8 and 14 units, respectively, and the area of \(\triangle LMR\) is 36 square units. Substituting 14 for \(RP\), 8 for \(MR\), and 36 for \(\text{ area of } \triangle LMR\) in the equation \(\frac{\text{ area of } \triangle PQR}{\text{ area of } \triangle LMR}= (\frac{RP}{MR})^{2}\) yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/b5d62bba_m0.webp" alt="">, which is equivalent to <img class="minl" src="/qimg/b5d62bba_m1.webp" alt=""> , or \(\frac{\text{ area of } \triangle PQR}{36}=\frac{49}{16}\) . Multiplying each side of this equation by 36 yields \(\text{ area of } \triangle PQR=36(\frac{49}{16})\) , or \(\text{ area of } \triangle PQR =\frac{441}{4}\) . Therefore, the area of \(\triangle PQR\) is \(\frac{441}{4}\) square units.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the result of multiplying 36 by \((\frac{8}{14})^{2}\) instead of by \((\frac{14}{8})^{2}\) .</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the result of multiplying 36 by \(\frac{8}{14}\) instead of by \((\frac{14}{8})^{2}\).</p><p>2</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the result of multiplying 36 by \(\frac{14}{8}\) instead of by \((\frac{14}{8})^{2}\).</p>' WHERE id='b5d62bba';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A right rectangular prism has a base area of \(24t\text{ square centimeters } (cm^{2})\) . The length of the base of the rectangular prism is \(\frac{8}{3}cm\), and the height of the rectangular prism is \(15 cm\). Which expression represents the surface area, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/62ec6574_m0.webp" alt=""> , of the right rectangular prism?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(48t + 160\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(318t + 80\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(1,968t + 80\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(360t\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that the base area of the right rectangular prism is \(24t cm^{2}\) and the length of the base is \(\frac{8}{3}cm\). Dividing the base area by the length yields the width: \(\frac{24t}{8}\) , or \((^{24t})(\frac{3}{8})\) , or \(9t cm\). It''s also given that the height of the prism is \(15 cm\). A right rectangular prism 3 has two rectangular bases and four rectangular lateral faces. The total area of the two bases is \(2(24t)\) , or \(48t cm^{2}\) . The four lateral faces include two with dimensions \(\frac{8}{3}cm\) by \(15 cm\) and two with dimensions \(9t cm\) by \(15 cm\). The total area of these four lateral faces is \(^{2}(\frac{8}{3})(15)+2(9t)(15) cm^{2}\), which is equivalent to \(80 + 270t cm^{2}\). Adding this to the total area of the two bases yields \(48t + 270t + 80\), or \(318t + 80 cm^{2}\) . Thus, the expression, which represents the surface area, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/62ec6574_m1.webp" alt="">, of the right rectangular prism is \(318t + 80\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the volume, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/62ec6574_m2.webp" alt="">, not the surface area, in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/62ec6574_m3.webp" alt="">.</p>' WHERE id='62ec6574';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where angle \(A\) corresponds to angle \(D\), and angles \(C\) and \(F\) are right angles. If \(\cos B =\frac{1}{22}\) , what is the value of \(\cos E\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{22}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{23}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{21}{22}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{22}{23}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. The cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the leg adjacent to that angle to the length of the hypotenuse. It''s given that angle \(C\) is a right angle in triangle \(ABC\) and that angle F is a right angle in triangle \(DEF\). Therefore, \(\cos B\) is equal to the ratio of the length of side \(BC\) to the length of side \(AB\), and \(\cos E\) is equal to the ratio of the length of side \(EF\) to the length of side \(DE\). It’s also given that triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where angle \(A\) corresponds to angle \(D\). Since similar triangles have proportional side lengths, \(\frac{BC}{AB}=\frac{EF}{DE}\) . Therefore, the value of \(\cos B\) is equal to the value of \(\cos E\). Since \(\cos B =\frac{1}{22}\) , the value of \(\cos E\) is \(\frac{1}{22}\) .</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>' WHERE id='bbaac300';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What is the value of \(\cos \frac{565\pi }{6}\) ?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>1</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\sqrt{3}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The cosine of an angle is equal to the cosine of \(n(2\pi )\) radians more than the angle, where \(n\) is an integer constant. Since \(\frac{565\pi }{6}\) is equivalent to \(47(2\pi )+\frac{\pi }{6}\) , \(\cos (\frac{565\pi }{6})\) can be rewritten as \(\cos (47(2\pi )+\frac{\pi }{6})\) , which is equal to \(\cos (\frac{\pi }{6})\) . Therefore, the value of \(\cos (\frac{565\pi }{6})\) is equal to the value of \(\cos (\frac{\pi }{6})\) , which is \(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\) .</p><p>Alternate approach: A trigonometric ratio can be found using the unit circle, that is, a circle with radius 1 unit. The cosine of a number \(t\) is the x- coordinate of the point resulting from traveling a distance of \(t\) counterclockwise from the point (1,0) around a unit circle centered at the origin in the xy-plane. A unit circle has a circumference of 2π. It follows that since \(\frac{565\pi }{6}\) is equal to \(47(2\pi )+\frac{\pi }{6}\) , traveling a distance of \(\frac{565\pi }{6}\) counterclockwise around a unit circle means traveling around the circle completely 47 times and then another \(\frac{\pi }{6}\) beyond that. That is, traveling \(\frac{565\pi }{6}\) results in the same point as traveling \(\frac{\pi }{6}\) . Traveling \(\frac{\pi }{6}\) counterclockwise from the point (1,0) around a unit circle centered at the origin in the xy-plane results in the point \((\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac{1}{2})\) . Thus, the value of \(\cos \frac{565\pi }{6}\) is the x-coordinate of the point \((\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac{1}{2})\) , which is \(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the value of \(\sin \frac{565\pi }{6}\) , not \(\cos \frac{565\pi }{6}\) .</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of the cosine of a multiple of 2π, not \(\frac{565\pi }{6}\) .</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of <img class="minl" src="/qimg/f2495de4_m0.webp" alt=""> , not \(\cos \frac{565\pi }{6}\) . 6</p>' WHERE id='f2495de4';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>In right triangle \(XYZ\), angles \(X\) and \(Z\) are acute angles. Point \(R\) lies on \(XZ\) such that \(YR\) is perpendicular to \(XZ\). The measure of \(∠XZY\) is</p><h3>Prompt</h3><ul><li>\(a^{\circ}\), the measure of \(∠XYR\) is \(b^{\circ}\), and the length of \(XY\) is 19. If \(\sin a^{\circ}=\frac{12}{13}\) , what is the value of \(\tan b^{\circ}\)?</li></ul>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["2.4, 12/5"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{12}{5}\) . It''s given that in right triangle \(XYZ\), \(∠X\) and \(∠Z\) are acute angles. It follows that \(∠Y\) is a right angle. It''s given that point \(R\) lies on \(XZ\) such that \(YR\) is perpendicular to \(XZ\). Since \(∠Y\) is a right angle and is comprised of \(∠XYR\) and \(∠ZYR\), it follows that the sum of the measures of \(∠XYR\) and \(∠ZYR\) is 90°. It''s given that the measure of \(∠XYR\) is \(b^{\circ}\). It follows that the measure of \(∠ZYR\) is</p><ul><li>\((90-b)^{\circ}\). Since \(YR\) is perpendicular to \(XZ\), it follows that \(∠XRY\) and \(∠ZRY\) are right angles. It follows that <img class="minl" src="/qimg/98e02472_m0.webp" alt=""> and <img class="minl" src="/qimg/98e02472_m1.webp" alt=""> are right triangles. It''s given that the measure of \(∠XZY\) is \(a^{\circ}\). Because the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is 180°, it follows that the measures of \(∠ZRY\), \(∠XZY\), and \(∠ZYR\) have a sum of 180°. Since the measure of \(∠ZRY\) is 90°, the measure of \(∠YZR\) is \(a^{\circ}\), and the measure of \(∠ZYR\) is \((90-b)^{\circ}\), it follows that \(90 + a+ 90 - b= 180\), or \(a - b+ 180 = 180\). Subtracting 180 from both sides of this equation yields \(a - b= 0\). Adding \(b\) to both sides of this equation yields \(a = b\). It''s given that \(\sin a^{\circ}=\frac{12}{13}\) . Since \(a = b\), it follows that \(\sin b^{\circ}\) \(=\frac{12}{13}\) . In a right triangle, the sine of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. It follows that, for a positive constant \(k\), \(12k\) represents the length of the leg opposite \(∠XYR\) and \(13k\) represents the length of the hypotenuse of \(\triangle XRY\). The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. Let \(nk\) represent the length of the leg adjacent to \(∠XYR\). It follows that \((13k)^{2}= (12k)^{2}+ (nk)^{2}\), or \(169k^{2}= 144k^{2}+ n^{2}k^{2}\). Subtracting \(144k^{2}\) from both sides of this equation yields \(25k^{2}= n^{2}k^{2}\). Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields \(5k = nk\). Therefore, the length of the leg adjacent to \(∠XYR\) is \(5k\). In a right triangle, the tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that \(\tan b^{\circ}=\frac{12k}{5k}\) , or \(\tan b^{\circ}=\frac{12}{5}\) . Note that 12/5 and 2.4 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</li></ul>' WHERE id='98e02472';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(RS = 440\)</p><p>\(ST = 384\)</p><p>\(TR = 584\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>The side lengths of right triangle \(RST\) are given. Triangle \(RST\) is similar to triangle \(UVw\), where \(S\) corresponds to \(V\) and \(T\) corresponds to \(w\). What is the value of \(\tan w\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{48}{73}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{55}{73}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{48}{55}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{55}{48}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. The hypotenuse of triangle \(RST\) is the longest side and is across from the right angle. The longest side length given is 584, which is the length of side \(TR\). Therefore, the hypotenuse of triangle \(RST\) is side \(TR\), so the right angle is angle \(S\). The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side, which is the side across from the angle, to the length of the adjacent side, which is the side closest to the angle that is not the hypotenuse. It follows that the opposite side of angle \(T\) is side \(RS\) and the adjacent side of angle \(T\) is side \(ST\). Therefore, \(\tan T =\frac{RS}{ST}\) . Substituting 440 for \(RS\) and 384 for \(ST\) in this equation yields \(\tan T =\frac{440}{384}\) . This is equivalent to \(\tan T =\frac{55}{48}\) . It’s given that triangle \(RST\) is similar to triangle \(UVw\), where \(S\) corresponds to \(V\) and \(T\) corresponds to \(w\). It follows that \(R\) corresponds to \(U\). Therefore, the hypotenuse of triangle \(UVw\) is side \(wU\), which means \(\tan w =\frac{UV}{Vw}\) . Since the lengths of corresponding sides of similar triangles are proportional, \(\frac{RS}{ST}=\frac{UV}{Vw}\) . Therefore, \(\tan w =\frac{UV}{Vw}\) is equivalent to \(\tan w =\frac{RS}{ST}\) , or \(\tan w = \tan T\). Thus, \(\tan w =\frac{55}{48}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the value of \(\cos w\), not \(\tan w\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of \(\sin w\), not \(\tan w\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of <img class="minl" src="/qimg/0e709a29_m0.webp" alt=""> , not \(\tan w\).</p>' WHERE id='0e709a29';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/a6097ec2_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In the right triangle shown, what is the value of \(\sin x^{\circ}\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{73}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{10}{73}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{63}{73}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{136}{73}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The sine of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the side opposite that angle to the length of the hypotenuse. In the right triangle shown, it''s given that the length of the side opposite the angle with measure \(x^{\circ}\) is 63 units and the length of the hypotenuse is 73 units. Therefore, the value of \(\sin x^{\circ}\) is \(\frac{63}{73}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='a6097ec2';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/eeb4143c_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>The area of triangle ABC above is at least 48 but no more than 60. If y is an integer, what is one possible value of x ?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["10/3, 15/4, 25/6"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is either <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m0.webp" alt="">, <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m1.webp" alt="">, or <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m2.webp" alt="">. The area of triangle ABC can be expressed as <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m3.webp" alt=""> or <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m4.webp" alt="">. It’s given that the area of</p><p>triangle ABC is at least 48 but no more than 60. It follows that <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m5.webp" alt="">. Dividing by 6 to isolate y in this compound inequality yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m6.webp" alt="">. Since y is an integer, <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m7.webp" alt="">. In the given figure, the two right triangles shown are similar because they have two pairs</p><p>of congruent angles: their respective right angles and angle A. Therefore, the following proportion is true: <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m8.webp" alt="">. Substituting 8 for y in the</p><p>proportion results in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m9.webp" alt="">. Cross multiplying and solving for x yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m10.webp" alt="">. Substituting 9 for y in the proportion results in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m11.webp" alt="">.</p><p>Cross multiplying and solving for x yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m12.webp" alt="">. Substituting 10 for y in the proportion results in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m13.webp" alt="">. Cross multiplying and solving for</p><p>x yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/eeb4143c_m14.webp" alt="">. Note that 10/3, 15/4, 25/6, 3.333, 3.75, 4.166, and 4.167 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='eeb4143c';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A right rectangular prism has a length of \(28\text{ centimeters } (cm)\), a width of \(15 cm\), and a height of \(16 cm\). What is the surface area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\), of the right rectangular prism?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["2216"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 2,216. The surface area of a prism is the sum of the areas of all its faces. A right rectangular prism consists of six rectangular faces, where opposite faces are congruent. It''s given that this prism has a length of \(28 cm\), a width of \(15 cm\), and a height of \(16 cm\). Thus, for this prism, there are two faces with area \((28) (15) cm^{2}\) , two faces with area \((28) (16) cm^{2}\) , and two faces with area \((15) (16) cm^{2}\). Therefore, the surface area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\) , of the right rectangular prism is 2(28) (15)+2(28) (16) +2(15) (16) , or 2,216.</p>' WHERE id='9f934297';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What is the area, in square centimeters, of a rectangle with a length of \(34\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) and a width of \(29 cm\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["986"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 986. The area, \(A\), of a rectangle is given by Math output error, where Math output error is the length of the rectangle and \(w\) is its width. It’s given that the length of the rectangle is 34 centimeters (cm) and the width is 29 cm. Substituting 34 for Math output error and 29 for \(w\) in the equation Math output error yields \(A= (34) (29)\) , or \(A = 986\). Therefore, the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle is 986.</p>' WHERE id='575f1e12';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An angle has a measure of \(\frac{9\pi }{20}\) radians. What is the measure of the angle in degrees?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["81"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 81 . The measure of an angle, in degrees, can be found by multiplying its measure, in radians, by \(\frac{180\text{ degrees}}{\pi\text{ radians}}\) . Multiplying the given angle measure, \(\frac{9\pi }{20}\) radians, by \(\frac{180\text{ degrees}}{\pi\text{ radians}}\) yields \((\frac{9\pi }{20}\text{ radians})(\frac{180\text{ degrees}}{\pi\text{ radians}})\) , which is equivalent to 81 degrees.</p>' WHERE id='74d8b897';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>An angle has a measure of \(\frac{16\pi }{15}\) radians. What is the measure of the angle, in degrees?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["192"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 192. The measure of an angle, in degrees, can be found by multiplying its measure, in radians, by \(\frac{180\text{ degrees}}{\pi\text{ radians}}\) . Multiplying the given angle measure, \(\frac{16\pi }{15}\text{ radians}\), by \(\frac{180\text{ degrees}}{\pi\text{ radians}}\) yields \((\frac{16\pi }{15}\text{ radians})(\frac{180\text{ degrees}}{\pi\text{ radians}})\) , which simplifies to 192 degrees.</p>' WHERE id='a0cacec1';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(RS = 20\)</p><p>\(ST = 48\)</p><p>\(TR = 52\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>The side lengths of right triangle \(RST\) are given. Triangle \(RST\) is similar to triangle \(UVw\), where \(S\) corresponds to \(V\) and \(T\) corresponds to \(w\). What is the value of \(\tan w\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{5}{13}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{5}{12}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{12}{13}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{12}{5}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It''s given that right triangle \(RST\) is similar to triangle \(UVw\), where \(S\) corresponds to \(V\) and \(T\) corresponds to \(w\). It''s given that the side lengths of the right triangle \(RST\) are \(RS = 20\), \(ST = 48\), and \(TR = 52\). Corresponding angles in similar triangles are equal. It follows that the measure of angle \(T\) is equal to the measure of angle \(w\). The hypotenuse of a right triangle is the longest side. It follows that the hypotenuse of triangle \(RST\) is side \(TR\). The hypotenuse of a right triangle is the side opposite the right angle. Therefore, angle \(S\) is a right angle. The adjacent side of an acute angle in a right triangle is the side closest to the angle that is not the hypotenuse. It follows that the adjacent side of angle \(T\) is side \(ST\). The opposite side of an acute angle in a right triangle is the side across from the acute angle. It follows that the opposite side of angle \(T\) is side \(RS\). The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. Therefore, \(\tan T =\frac{RS}{ST}\) . Substituting 20 for \(RS\) and 48 for \(ST\) in this equation yields \(\tan T =\frac{20}{48}\) , or \(\tan T\) \(=\frac{5}{12}\) . The tangents of two acute angles with equal measures are equal. Since the measure of angle \(T\) is equal to the measure of angle \(w\), it follows that \(\tan T = \tan w\). Substituting \(\frac{5}{12}\) for \(\tan T\) in this equation yields \(\frac{5}{12}= \tan w\). Therefore, the value of \(\tan w\) is \(\frac{5}{12}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the value of \(\sin w\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of \(\cos w\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{1}{\tan w}\) .</p>' WHERE id='c9931030';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where \(A\) corresponds to \(D\) and \(C\) corresponds to \(F\). Angles \(C\) and \(F\) are right angles. If \(\tan (A)\) \(=\sqrt{3}\) and \(DF = 125\), what is the length of \(DE\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(125\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(125\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(125\\sqrt{3}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>250</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. Corresponding angles in similar triangles have equal measures. It''s given that triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where \(A\) corresponds to \(D\), so the measure of angle \(A\) is equal to the measure of angle \(D\). Therefore, if \(\tan (A) =\sqrt{3}\), then \(\tan (D) =\sqrt{3}\). It''s given that angles \(C\) and \(F\) are right angles, so triangles \(ABC\) and \(DEF\) are right triangles. The adjacent side of an acute angle in a right triangle is the side closest to the angle that is not the hypotenuse. It follows that the adjacent side of angle \(D\) is side \(DF\). The opposite side of an acute angle in a right triangle is the side across from the acute angle. It follows that the opposite side of angle \(D\) is side \(EF\). The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. Therefore, \(\tan (D) =\frac{EF}{DF}\) . If \(DF = 125\), the length of side \(EF\) can be found by substituting \(\sqrt{3}\) for \(\tan (D)\) and 125 for \(DF\) in the equation \(\tan (D) =\frac{EF}{DF}\) , which yields \(\sqrt{3}=\frac{EF}{125}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by 125 yields \(125\sqrt{3}= EF\). Since the length of side \(EF\) is \(\sqrt{3}\) times the length of side \(DF\), it follows that triangle \(DEF\) is a special right triangle with angle measures 30°, 60°, and 90°. Therefore, the length of the hypotenuse, \(DE\), is 2 times the length of side \(DF\), or \(DE= 2(DF)\) . Substituting 125 for \(DF\) in this equation yields \(DE= 2(125)\) , or \(DE = 250\). Thus, if \(\tan (A) =\sqrt{3}\) and \(DF = 125\), the length of \(DE\) is 250.</p><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/6ab30ce3_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the length of \(EF\), not \(DE\).</p>' WHERE id='6ab30ce3';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/e9c5bfb2_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/e9c5bfb2_fig1.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/e9c5bfb2_m0.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>Rectangles \(ABCD\) and \(QRST\) shown are similar, where \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\) correspond to \(Q\), \(R\), \(S\), and \(T\), respectively. What is the length, in inches ( in) , of \(AD\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>60</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>24</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>17</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>10</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. It’s given that rectangles \(ABCD\) and \(QRST\) are similar, where \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\) correspond to \(Q\), \(R\), \(S\), and \(T\), respectively. It follows that \(AB\) corresponds to \(QR\) and \(AD\) corresponds to \(QT\). If two rectangles are similar, then the lengths of their corresponding sides are proportional. It’s given in the figure that the length of \(AB\) is 12 inches, the length of \(QR\) is 5 inches, and the length of \(QT\) is 10 inches. If \(x\) is the length, in inches, of \(AD\), then \(\frac{12}{5}\) is equivalent to \(\frac{x}{10}\) . Therefore, the value of \(x\) can be found using the equation \(\frac{12}{5}=\frac{x}{10}\) . Multiplying each side of this equation by 10 yields \(\frac{120}{5}= x\), or \(24 = x\). Therefore, the length, in inches, of \(AD\) is 24.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of \(QT\), not \(AD\).</p>' WHERE id='e9c5bfb2';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What is the value of \(\tan \frac{92\pi }{3}\) ?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(-\\sqrt{3}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\sqrt{3}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. A trigonometric ratio can be found using the unit circle, that is, a circle with radius 1 unit. If a central angle of a unit circle in the xy-plane centered at the origin has its starting side on the positive x-axis and its terminal side intersects the circle at a point \((x,y)\) , then the value of the tangent of the central angle is equal to the y-coordinate divided by the x-coordinate. There are 2π radians in a circle. Dividing \(\frac{92\pi }{3}\) by 2π yields \(\frac{92}{6}\) , which is equivalent to \(15 +\frac{2}{3}\) . It follows that on the unit circle centered at the origin in the xy-plane, the angle \(\frac{92\pi }{3}\) is the result of 15 revolutions from its starting side on the positive x-axis followed by a rotation through \(\frac{2\pi }{3}\) radians. Therefore, the angles \(\frac{92\pi }{3}\) and \(\frac{2\pi }{3}\) are coterminal angles and \(\tan (\frac{92\pi }{3})\) is equal to \(\tan (\frac{2\pi }{3})\) . Since \(\frac{2\pi }{3}\) is greater than \(\frac{\pi }{2}\) and less than π, it follows that the terminal side of the angle is in quadrant II and forms an angle of \(\frac{\pi }{3}\) , or 60°, with the negative x-axis. Therefore, the terminal side of the angle intersects the unit circle at the √3 point \((-\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2})\) . It follows that the value of \(\tan (\frac{2\pi }{3})\) is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/244ff6c4_m0.webp" alt=""> , which is equivalent to \(-\sqrt{3}\). Therefore, the value of \(\tan (\frac{92\pi }{3})\) is \(-\sqrt{3}\). 2</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{1}{\tan (^{92\pi })}\) , not \(\tan (\frac{92\pi }{3})\) . 3</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of \(\frac{1}{\tan (^{\pi })}\) , not \(\tan (\frac{92\pi }{3})\) . 3</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of \(\tan (\frac{\pi }{3})\) , not \(\tan (\frac{92\pi }{3})\) .</p>' WHERE id='244ff6c4';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A triangular prism has a height of \(8\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) and a volume of \(216 cm^{3}\). What is the area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\), of the base of the prism? (The volume of a triangular prism is equal to \(Bh\), where \(B\) is the area of the base and \(h\) is the height of the prism.)</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["27"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 27. It''s given that a triangular prism has a volume of \(216\text{ cubic centimeters } (cm^{3})\) and the volume of a triangular prism is equal to \(Bh\), where \(B\) is the area of the base and \(h\) is the height of the prism. Therefore, \(216 = Bh\). It''s also given that the triangular prism has a height of \(8 cm\). Therefore, \(h = 8\). Substituting 8 for \(h\) in the equation \(216 = Bh\) yields \(216= B(8)\) . Dividing both sides of this equation by 8 yields \(27 = B\). Therefore, the area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\) , of the base of the prism is 27.</p>' WHERE id='08b7a3f5';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Right rectangular prism P is similar to right rectangular prism Q. The volume of prism P is \(972\text{ cubic centimeters } (cm^{3})\) , and the volume of prism Q is \(36 cm^{3}\). One edge of prism P has a length of \(12 cm\). What is the length, in \(cm\), of the corresponding edge of prism Q?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["4"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 4. It’s given that right rectangular prism P is similar to right rectangular prism Q. Therefore, there is a positive constant \(k\) such that the length of each edge of prism P is \(k\) times the length of the corresponding edge of prism Q and the volume of prism P is \(k^{3}\) times the volume of prism Q. It’s given that the volume of prism P is \(972 cm^{3}\) and the volume of prism Q is \(36 cm^{3}\) . It follows that \(972 = 36k^{3}\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 36 yields \(\frac{972}{36}= k^{3}\), or \(k^{3}= 27\). Taking the cube root of both sides of this equation yields \(k = 3\). It follows that the length of each edge of prism P is 3 times the length of the corresponding edge of prism Q. It’s given that one edge of prism P has a length of \(12 cm\). It follows that \(12 cm\) is 3 times the length of the corresponding edge of prism Q. Thus, the length, in \(cm\), of the edge of prism Q corresponding to the edge of prism P with a length of \(12 cm\) is \(\frac{12}{3}\) , or 4.</p>' WHERE id='73e1b793';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>A circle has center \(O\), and points \(A\) and \(B\) lie on the circle. The measure of arc \(AB\) is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/ca2235f6_m0.webp" alt=""> and the length of arc \(AB\) is 3 inches. What is the circumference, in inches, of the circle?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>3</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>6</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>9</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>24</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It’s given that the measure of arc \(AB\) is 45° and the length of arc \(AB\) is \(3\text{ inches}\). The arc measure of the full circle is 360°. If \(x\) represents the circumference, in inches, of the circle, it follows that \(\frac{45^{\circ}}{360^{\circ}}=\frac{3\text{ inches}}{x\text{ inches}}\) . This equation is equivalent to \(\frac{45}{360}=\frac{3}{x}\) , or \(\frac{1}{8}=\frac{3}{x}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by \(8x\) yields \(1(x) = 3(8)\) , or \(x = 24\). Therefore, the circumference of the circle is \(24\text{ inches}\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the length of arc \(AB\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from multiplying the length of arc \(AB\) by 2.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from squaring the length of arc \(AB\).</p>' WHERE id='ca2235f6';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of \(90\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) . The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is \(K cm^{2}\) . If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of \(\frac{92}{47}K cm^{2}\) . What is the side length, in \(cm\), of each square base?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>4</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>8</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>9</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>16</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. Let \(x\) represent the side length, in \(cm\), of each square base. If the two prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area equal to twice the surface area of one of the prisms, minus the area of the two square bases that are being glued together, which yields \(2K- 2x^{2}cm^{2}\) . It’s given that this resulting surface area is equal to \(\frac{92}{47}K cm^{2}\), so \(2K- 2x^{2}=\frac{92}{47}K\). Subtracting \(\frac{92}{47}K\) from both sides of this equation yields \(2K -\frac{92}{47}K- 2x^{2}= 0\). This equation can be rewritten by multiplying \(2K\) on the left- hand side by \(\frac{47}{47}\) , which yields \(\frac{94}{47}K -\frac{92}{47}K- 2x^{2}= 0\), or \(\frac{2}{47}K - 2x^{2}= 0\). Adding \(2x^{2}\) to both sides of this equation yields \(\frac{2}{47}K = 2x^{2}\). Multiplying both sides of this equation by \(\frac{47}{2}\) yields \(K = 47x^{2}\) . The surface area \(K\), in <img class="minl" src="/qimg/f243c383_m0.webp" alt=""> , of each rectangular prism is equivalent to the sum of the areas of the two square bases and the areas of the four lateral faces. Since the height of each rectangular prism is \(90 cm\) and the side length of each square base is \(x cm\), it follows that the area of each square base is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/f243c383_m1.webp" alt=""> and the area of each lateral face is \(90x cm^{2}\). Therefore, the surface area of each rectangular prism can be represented by the expression \(2x^{2}+4(90x)\) , or \(2x^{2}+ 360x\). Substituting this expression for \(K\) in the equation \(K = 47x^{2}\) yields \(2x^{2}+ 360x = 47x^{2}\). Subtracting \(2x^{2}\) and \(360x\) from both sides of this equation yields 0 \(= 45x^{2}- 360x\). Factoring \(x\) from the right-hand side of this equation yields \(0 = x(45x-360)\). Applying the zero product property, it follows that \(x = 0\) and \(45x - 360 = 0\). Adding 360 to both sides of the equation \(45x - 360 = 0\) yields \(45x = 360\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 45 yields \(x = 8\). Since a side length of a rectangular prism can’t be 0, the length of each square base is \(8 cm\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='f243c383';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Which of the following expressions is equivalent to \((\sin 24^{\circ})(\cos 66^{\circ}) + (\cos 24^{\circ})(\sin 66^{\circ})\) ?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<p>\\(2(\\cos 66^{\\circ})(\\sin 24^{\\circ})\\)</p>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<p>\\(2(\\cos 66^{\\circ})+2(\\cos 24^{\\circ})\\)</p>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<p>\\((\\cos 66^{\\circ})^{2}+ (\\cos 24^{\\circ})^{2}\\)</p>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<p>\\((\\cos 66^{\\circ})^{2}+ (\\sin 24^{\\circ})^{2}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The sine of an angle is equal to the cosine of its complementary angle. Since angles with measures 24° and 66° are complementary to each other, \(\sin 24^{\circ}\) is equal to \(\cos 66^{\circ}\) and \(\sin 66^{\circ}\) is equal to \(\cos 24^{\circ}\). Substituting \(\cos 66^{\circ}\) for \(\sin 24^{\circ}\) and \(\cos 24^{\circ}\) for</p><ul><li>\(\sin 66^{\circ}\) in the given expression yields \((\cos 66^{\circ})(\cos 66^{\circ}) + (\cos 24^{\circ})(\cos 24^{\circ})\), or \((\cos 66^{\circ})^{2}+ (\cos 24^{\circ})^{2}\).</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='16d66178';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/f1251f35_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In triangle \(XYZ\), \(\cos Z =\frac{560}{c}\) . What is the value of \(c\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["565"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 565. In triangle \(XYZ\), angle \(Y\) is a right angle. Therefore, triangle \(XYZ\) is a right triangle. The cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the side adjacent to that angle to the length of the hypotenuse. In right triangle \(XYZ\), side \(YZ\) is the side adjacent to angle \(Z\) and side \(XZ\) is the hypotenuse. It’s given that the length of side \(YZ\) is 560 and the length of side \(XZ\) is 565. Therefore, \(\cos Z =\frac{560}{565}\) . It''s also given that \(\cos Z =\frac{560}{c}\) . It follows that \(\frac{560}{c}=\frac{560}{565}\) . Therefore, \(c = 565\).</p>' WHERE id='f1251f35';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What is the area of a rectangle with a length of \(17\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) and a width of \(7 cm\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(24 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(48 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(119 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(576 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The area of a rectangle with length <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d2047497_m0.webp" alt=""> and width \(w\) can be found using the formula <img class="minl" src="/qimg/d2047497_m1.webp" alt="">. It’s given that the rectangle has a length of \(17 cm\) and a width of \(7 cm\). Therefore, the area of this rectangle is \(A= 17(7)\) , or \(119 cm^{2}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the sum of the length and width of the rectangle, not the area.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the perimeter of the rectangle, not the area.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the sum of the length and width of the rectangle squared, not the area.</p>' WHERE id='d2047497';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>Points A and B lie on a circle with radius 1, and arc <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m0.webp" alt=""> has length <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m1.webp" alt="">. What fraction of the circumference of the circle is the length of arc <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m2.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["1/6"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m3.webp" alt="">. The circumference, C, of a circle is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m4.webp" alt="">, where r is the length of the radius of the circle. For the given circle with</p><p>a radius of 1, the circumference is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m5.webp" alt="">, or <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m6.webp" alt="">. To find what fraction of the circumference the length of arc <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m7.webp" alt=""> is, divide the</p><p>length of the arc by the circumference, which gives <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m8.webp" alt="">. This division can be represented by <img class="minl" src="/qimg/fb58c0db_m9.webp" alt="">. Note that 1/6, .1666,</p><p>.1667, 0.166, and 0.167 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='fb58c0db';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where angle \(A\) corresponds to angle \(D\) and angles \(C\) and \(F\) are right angles. The length of \(AB\) is 2.9 times the length of \(DE\). If \(\tan A =\frac{21}{20}\) , what is the value of \(\sin D\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='[".7241, 21/29"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{21}{29}\) . It''s given that triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where angle \(A\) corresponds to angle \(D\) and angles \(C\) and \(F\) are right angles. In similar triangles, the tangents of corresponding angles are equal. Therefore, if \(\tan A =\frac{21}{20}\) , then \(\tan D =\frac{21}{20}\) . In a right triangle, the tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. Therefore, in triangle \(DEF\), if \(\tan D =\frac{21}{20}\) , the ratio of the length of \(EF\) to the length of \(DF\) is \(\frac{21}{20}\) . If the lengths of \(EF\) and \(DF\) are 21 and 20, respectively, then the ratio of the length of \(EF\) to the length of \(DF\) is \(\frac{21}{20}\) . In a right triangle, the sine of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. Therefore, the value of \(\sin D\) is the ratio of the length of \(EF\) to the length of \(DE\). The length of \(DE\) can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem, which states that if the lengths of the legs of a right triangle are \(a\) and \(b\) and the length of the hypotenuse is \(c\), then \(a^{2}+ b^{2}= c^{2}\) . Therefore, if the lengths of \(EF\) and \(DF\) are 21 and 20, respectively, then \((21)^{2}\) \(+ (20)^{2}= (DE)^{2}\) , or \(841 = (DE)^{2}\). Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields \(29 = DE\). Therefore, if the lengths of \(EF\) and \(DF\) are 21 and 20, respectively, then the length of \(DE\) is 29 and the ratio of the length of \(EF\) to the length of \(DE\) is \(\frac{21}{29}\) . Thus, if \(\tan A =\frac{21}{20}\) , the value of \(\sin D\) is \(\frac{21}{29}\) . Note that 21/29, .7241, and 0.724 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='c6dff223';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What is the area of a rectangle with a length of \(4\text{ centimeters } (cm)\) and a width of \(2 cm\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(6 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(8 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(12 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(36 cm^{2}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. The area of a rectangle with length ℓ and width \(w\) can be found using the formula \(A = ℓw\). It’s given that the rectangle has a length of \(4 cm\) and a width of \(2 cm\). Therefore, the area of this rectangle is \((4 cm)(2 cm)\), or \(8 cm^{2}\).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the sum, in cm, of the length and width of the rectangle, not the area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the perimeter, in cm, of the rectangle, not the area, \(\text{ in } cm^{2}\).</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the sum of the length and width of the rectangle squared, not the area.</p>' WHERE id='4420e500';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/a67b9f88_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In the triangle shown, \(RS =\sqrt{105}\). What is the value of \(\sin R\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='[".9811, 52/53"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{52}{53}\) . In a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the two legs. The length of the hypotenuse of the right triangle shown is 53. It’s given that \(RS =\sqrt{105}\). Therefore, the length of one of the legs of 2 the triangle shown is \(\sqrt{105}\). Let \(x\) represent \(TS\), the length of the other leg of the triangle shown. Therefore, \(53^{2}=(\sqrt{105}) +x^{2}\), or 2,809 \(= 105 + x^{2}\) . Subtracting 105 from both sides of this equation yields \(2,704 = x^{2}\). Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields \(52 = x\). Therefore, \(TS\), the length of the other leg of the triangle shown, is 52. The sine of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. The length of the leg opposite angle \(R\) is 52, and the length of the hypotenuse is 53. Therefore, the value of \(\sin R\) is \(\frac{52}{53}\) . Note that 52/53 or .9811 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='a67b9f88';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where angle \(A\) corresponds to angle \(D\) and angle \(C\) corresponds to angle \(F\). Angles \(C\) and \(F\) are right angles. If \(\tan (A) =\frac{50}{7}\) , what is the value of \(\tan (E)\) ?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='[".14, 7/50"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{7}{50}\) . It''s given that triangle \(ABC\) is similar to triangle \(DEF\), where angle \(A\) corresponds to angle \(D\) and angle \(C\) corresponds to angle \(F\). In similar triangles, the tangents of corresponding angles are equal. Since angle \(A\) and angle \(D\) are corresponding angles, if \(\tan (A) =\frac{50}{7}\) , then \(\tan (D) =\frac{50}{7}\) . It''s also given that angles \(C\) and \(F\) are right angles. It follows that triangle \(DEF\) is a right triangle with acute angles \(D\) and \(E\). The tangent of one acute angle in a right triangle is the inverse of the tangent of the other acute angle in the triangle. Therefore, \(\tan (E) =\frac{1}{\tan (D)}\) . Substituting \(\frac{50}{7}\) for \(\tan (D)\) in this equation yields \(\tan (E) =\frac{1}{50}\) , or \(\tan (E) =\frac{7}{50}\) . Thus, if \(\tan (A) =\frac{50}{7}\) , the 7 value of \(\tan (E)\) is \(\frac{7}{50}\) . Note that 7/50 and .14 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='2be01bd9';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In triangle \(ABC\), \(AB= 4,680\text{ millimeters } (mm)\) and \(BC = 4,680 mm\). Which statement is sufficient to prove that triangle \(ABC\) is equilateral?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(AC = 4,680 mm\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(AC = 468 mm\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(AC = 46.8 mm\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(AC = 4.68 mm\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. In an equilateral triangle, all three sides have the same length. It’s given that in triangle \(ABC\), \(AB = 4,680 mm\) and \(BC\)</p><ul><li>\(= 4,680 mm\). Therefore, if \(AC = 4,680 mm\), then all three sides of triangle \(ABC\) have the same length, so triangle \(ABC\) is equilateral. Therefore, \(AC = 4,680 mm\) is sufficient to prove that triangle \(ABC\) is equilateral.</li></ul><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='739f1bbc';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/053e5e9f_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>In triangle \(QRS\) shown, \(QR&lt; RS\). Which expression represents the length of \(QS\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<p>\\(25\\cos Q\\)</p>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<p>\\(25\\sin Q\\)</p>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<p>\\(\\frac{25}{\\cos Q}\\)</p>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<p>\\(\\frac{25}{\\sin Q}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. The figure shows that triangle \(QRS\) is a right triangle. Each of the given choices is an expression containing \(\sin Q\) or \(\cos Q\). For an acute angle in a right triangle, the sine of the angle is the length of the opposite leg divided by the length of the hypotenuse, and the cosine of the angle is the length of the adjacent leg divided by the length of the hypotenuse. It follows that \(\sin Q =\frac{RS}{QS}\) and <img class="minl" src="/qimg/053e5e9f_m0.webp" alt=""> , where \(QR\) \(&lt; RS\). It''s given in the figure that the length of side \(RS\) is 25. Since only the equation \(\sin Q=\frac{RS}{QS}\) contains \(RS\), an expression representing \(QS\) can be found by substituting 25 for \(RS\) in this equation, which yields \(\sin Q =\frac{25}{QS}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by \(QS\) yields \(QS\)</p><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/053e5e9f_fig1.webp" alt="Figure"></div><ul><li>\(. \sin Q = 25\). Dividing both sides of this equation by \(\sin Q\) yields \(QS=\frac{25}{\sin Q}\) . Therefore, the expression \(\frac{25}{\sin Q}\) represents the length of \(QS\).</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='053e5e9f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In triangle \(JKL\), \(\cos (K) =\frac{24}{51}\) and angle \(J\) is a right angle. What is the value of \(\cos (L)\) ?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='[".8823, .8824, 15/17"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{15}{17}\) . It''s given that angle \(J\) is the right angle in triangle \(JKL\). Therefore, the acute angles of triangle \(JKL\) are angle \(K\) and angle \(L\). The hypotenuse of a right triangle is the side opposite its right angle. Therefore, the hypotenuse of triangle \(JKL\) is side \(KL\). The cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the side adjacent to the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. It''s given that \(\cos (K) =\frac{24}{51}\) . This can be written as \(\cos (K) =\frac{8}{17}\) . Since the cosine of angle \(K\) is a ratio, it follows that the length of the side adjacent to angle \(K\) is \(8n\) and the length of the hypotenuse is \(17n\), where \(n\) is a constant. Therefore, \(JK = 8n\) and \(KL = 17n\). The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. For triangle \(JKL\), it follows that \((JK)^{2}+ (JL)^{2}= (KL)^{2}\). Substituting \(8n\) for \(JK\) and \(17n\) for \(KL\) yields \((8n)^{2}+ (JL)^{2}= (17n)^{2}\) . This is equivalent to \(64n^{2}+ (JL)^{2}= 289n^{2}\) . Subtracting \(64n^{2}\) from each side of this equation yields \((JL)^{2}= 225n^{2}\). Taking the square root of each side of this equation yields \(JL = 15n\). Since \(\cos (L) =\frac{JL}{KL}\) , it follows that \(\cos (L) =\frac{15n}{17n}\) , which can be rewritten as \(\cos (L) =\frac{15}{17}\) . Note that 15/17, .8824, .8823, and 0.882 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='8027db3f';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>The measure of angle \(A\) is \(\frac{\pi }{216}\) radians. The measure of angle \(B\) is 36 times the measure of angle \(A\). What is the value of \(\cos B\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It’s given that the measure of angle \(A\) is \(\frac{\pi }{216}\) radians and the measure of angle \(B\) is 36 times the measure of angle \(A\). Therefore, the measure of angle \(B\) is \(36(\frac{\pi }{216})\) radians, or \(\frac{\pi }{6}\) radians. A trigonometric ratio can be found using the unit circle, or a circle with radius 1 unit. For a unit circle centered at the origin in the xy-plane, the cosine of \(\frac{\pi }{6}\) radians is the x-coordinate of the point resulting from traveling a distance of \(\frac{\pi }{6}\) counterclockwise from the point (1,0) . This results in the point \((\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac{1}{2})\) . Therefore, the value of \(\cos B\) is \(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of \(\sin B\), not \(\cos B\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='10e059a6';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/6c67dd47_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/6c67dd47_m0.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>In the figure shown, the measure of angle \(X\) is 54°. The length of \(XY\) is 26 units and the length of \(XZ\) is 19 units. What is the area, in square units, of triangle \(XYZ\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<p>247</p>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<p>494</p>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<p>\\(247\\sin 54^{\\circ}\\)</p>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<p>\\(494\\sin 54^{\\circ}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. The area, \(A\), of a triangle is given by the formula \(A =\frac{1}{2}bh\), where \(b\) is the length of a base of the triangle and \(h\) is its corresponding height. It’s given that the length of \(XY\) is 26 units. This length represents a base of the triangle, so \(b = 26\). Let \(h\) represent its corresponding height. It''s given that the measure of angle \(X\) is 54° and the length of \(XZ\) is 19 units. It follows that \(\sin 54^{\circ}=\frac{h}{19}\) . Multiplying both sides of this equation by 19 yields \(19\sin 54^{\circ}= h\). Substituting 26 for \(b\) and \(19 \sin 54^{\circ}\) for \(h\) in the formula \(A =\frac{1}{2}bh\) yields \(A\)</p><ul><li>\(=\frac{1}{2}(26)(19\sin 54^{\circ})\) , or \(A = 247\sin 54^{\circ}\). Therefore, the area, in square units, of triangle \(XYZ\) is \(247\sin 54^{\circ}\).</li></ul><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='6c67dd47';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/1bf809b5_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/1bf809b5_m0.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>In the triangle shown, what is the value of \(\cos x^{\circ}\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='[".3928, .3929, 11/28"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{11}{28}\) . The cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the leg adjacent to the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. In the triangle shown, the length of the leg adjacent to the angle with measure \(x^{\circ}\) is 11 units and the length of the hypotenuse is 28 units. Therefore, the value of \(\cos x^{\circ}\) is \(\frac{11}{28}\) . Note that 11/28, .3928, .3929, 0.392, and 0.393 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='1bf809b5';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>For two acute angles, \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\), \(\cos (Q) = \sin (R)\) . The measures, in degrees, of \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\) are \(x + 61\) and \(4x + 4\), respectively. What is the value of \(x\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>5</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>19</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>23</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>29</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. It''s given that for two acute angles, \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\), \(\cos (Q) = \sin (R)\) . For two acute angles, if the sine of one angle is equal to the cosine of the other angle, the angles are complementary. It follows that \(∠Q\) and \(∠R\) are complementary. That is, the sum of the measures of the angles is 90 degrees. It''s given that the measure of \(∠Q\) is \(x + 61\) degrees and the measure of \(∠R\) is \(4x + 4\) degrees. It follows that \((x+61) + (4x+4) = 90\). By combining like terms, this equation can be rewritten as \(5x + 65 = 90\). Subtracting 65 from each side of this equation yields \(5x = 25\). Dividing each side of this equation by 5 yields \(x = 5\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This would be the value of \(x\) if \(\cos (Q) = \cos (R)\) rather than \(\cos (Q) = \sin (R)\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This would be the value of \(x\) if \(\cos (Q) =-\cos (R)\) rather than \(\cos (Q) = \sin (R)\) and if \(∠R\) were obtuse rather than acute.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='14e7c1f4';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>Triangles \(ABC\) and \(DEF\) are similar. Each side length of triangle \(ABC\) is 4 times the corresponding side length of triangle \(DEF\). The area of triangle \(ABC\) is 270 square inches. What is the area, in square inches, of triangle \(DEF\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["135/8, 16.87, 16.88"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{135}{8}\) . It''s given that triangles \(ABC\) and \(DEF\) are similar and each side length of triangle \(ABC\) is 4 times the corresponding side length of triangle \(DEF\). For two similar triangles, if each side length of the first triangle is \(k\) times the corresponding side length of the second triangle, then the area of the first triangle is \(k^{2}\) times the area of the second triangle. Therefore, the area of triangle \(ABC\) is \(4^{2}\), or 16, times the area of triangle \(DEF\). It''s given that the area of triangle \(ABC\) is 270 square inches. Let \(a\) represent the area, in square inches, of triangle \(DEF\). It follows that 270 is 16 times \(a\), or \(270 = 16a\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 16 yields \(\frac{270}{16}= a\), which is equivalent to \(\frac{135}{8}= a\). Thus, the area, in square inches, of triangle \(DEF\) is \(\frac{135}{8}\) . Note that 135/8, 16.87, and 16.88 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='459dd6c5';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>In triangle \(XYZ\), the measure of angle \(X\) is 90°. Point \(w\) lies on segment \(YZ\), and segment \(wX\) is perpendicular to segment \(YZ\). The length of segment \(wY\) is 572, and the length of segment \(wX\) is 429. What is the value of \(\tan Z\)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{3}{5}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{3}{4}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{4}{5}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{4}{3}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["D"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice D is correct. It''s given that in triangle \(XYZ\), the measure of angle \(X\) is 90°, so triangle \(XYZ\) is a right triangle. It’s also given that point \(w\) lies on segment \(YZ\), and segment \(wX\) is perpendicular to segment \(YZ\). It follows that triangle \(wYX\) is a right triangle. Triangle \(wYX\) and triangle \(XYZ\) are right triangles with the same interior angle measure at point \(Y\). It follows that triangle \(wYX\) and triangle \(XYZ\) are similar, where angles \(w\), \(Y\), and \(X\) in triangle \(wYX\) correspond to angles \(X\), \(Y\), and \(Z\), respectively, in triangle \(XYZ\). Since corresponding angles in similar triangles are congruent, their tangents have equal value. Thus, the tangent of angle \(Z\) is equal to the tangent of angle \(X\) in triangle \(wYX\). The opposite side of angle \(X\) in triangle \(wYX\) is segment \(wY\), and the adjacent side is segment \(wX\). It’s given that the length of segment \(wY\) is 572 and the length of segment \(wX\) is 429. It follows that the tangent of angle \(X\) in triangle \(wYX\) is \(\frac{572}{429}\) , or \(\frac{4}{3}\) . Therefore, the value of \(\tan Z\) is \(\frac{4}{3}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the value of \(\cos Z\), not \(\tan Z\).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of \(\tan Y\), not \(\tan Z\).</p><p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of \(\sin Z\), not \(\tan Z\).</p>' WHERE id='603c18ad';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>What is the value of \(\sin 42\pi \)?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>0</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>1</p>"}]', correct_answer='["A"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice A is correct. The sine of a number \(t\) is the y-coordinate of the point arrived at by traveling a distance of \(t\) units counterclockwise around the unit circle from the starting point (1,0). Since the unit circle has a circumference of 2π units, it follows that one full rotation around the circle is equal to a distance of 2π units. Therefore, a distance of 42π units around the circle from the starting point (1,0) would result in exactly 21 full rotations, arriving back at the point (1,0) . So, \(\sin 42\pi \) is equal to the y-coordinate of the point (1,0) , which is 0.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of \(\cos 42\pi \), not \(\sin 42\pi \).</p>' WHERE id='1efd7ef3';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<p>If <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m0.webp" alt=""> for all positive values of x, what is the value of <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m1.webp" alt="">?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["7/6"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m2.webp" alt="">. The value of <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m3.webp" alt=""> can be found by first rewriting the left-hand side of the given equation as <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m4.webp" alt="">. Using the</p><p>properties of exponents, this expression can be rewritten as <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m5.webp" alt="">. This expression can be rewritten by subtracting the fractions in the</p><p>exponent, which yields <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m6.webp" alt="">. Thus, <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m7.webp" alt=""> is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/40c09d66_m8.webp" alt="">. Note that 7/6, 1.166, and 1.167 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>' WHERE id='40c09d66';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<div class="qfig"><img src="/qimg/1ee962ec_fig0.webp" alt="Figure"></div><p>Scientists recorded data about the ocean water levels at a certain location over a period of 6 hours. The graph shown models the data, where \(y = 0\) represents sea level. Which table gives values of \(x\) and their corresponding values of \(y\) based on the model?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter":"A","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>-12</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>6</td></tr></table></div>"},{"letter":"B","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>-6</td></tr></table></div>"},{"letter":"C","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>-12</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0</td></tr></table></div>"},{"letter":"D","content":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(y\\)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>-6</td><td>0</td></tr></table></div>"}]', correct_answer='["C"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice C is correct. Each point \((x,y)\) on the graph represents an elapsed time \(x\), in hours, and the corresponding ocean water level \(y\), in feet, at a certain location based on the model. The graph shown passes through the points (0,0) , (3,-12) , and (6,0). Thus, the table in choice C gives the values of \(x\) and their corresponding values of \(y\) based on the model.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='1ee962ec';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(4x^{2}- 5x - 5 = 0\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>What is the greatest solution to the given equation?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{5}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{105}}{8}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{\\sqrt{105}}{8}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{105}}{4}\\)</p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p>\\(\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{105}}{4}\\)</p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. The solutions to a quadratic equation of the form \(ax^{2}+ bx + c = 0\), where \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) are constants, can be calculated using the quadratic formula \(x =\frac{-b\pm \sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\) . The given equation is of this form, where \(a = 4\), \(b = -5\), and \(c = -5\). Substituting these values into the quadratic formula yields \(x =\frac{-(-5)\pm \sqrt{(-5)^{2}-4(4)(-5)}}{2(4)}\) , or \(x =\frac{5\pm \sqrt{25+80}}{8}\) . These solutions can be rewritten as \(x =\frac{5\pm \sqrt{105}}{8}\) , or \(x =\frac{5}{8}\) \(\pm \frac{\sqrt{105}}{8}\) . Since \(\frac{5}{8}+\frac{\sqrt{105}}{8}\) is greater than \(\frac{5}{8}-\frac{\sqrt{105}}{8}\) , it follows that the greatest solution to the given equation is \(\frac{5}{8}+\frac{\sqrt{105}}{8}\) .</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the least, not the greatest, solution to the given equation.</p><p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p><p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>' WHERE id='5da5c665';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(2x^{2}- 8x - 7 = 0\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>One solution to the given equation can be written as \(\frac{8-\sqrt{k}}{4}\) , where \(k\) is a constant. What is the value of \(k\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='["120"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is 120. The solutions to a quadratic equation of the form \(ax^{2}+ bx + c = 0\) can be calculated using the quadratic formula and are given by \(x =\frac{-b\pm \sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\) . The given equation is in the form \(ax^{2}+ bx + c = 0\), where \(a = 2\), \(b = -8\), and \(c = -7\). It follows that the solutions to the given equation are \(x =\frac{8\pm \sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4(2)(-7)}}{2(2)}\) , which is equivalent to \(x =\frac{8\pm \sqrt{64+56}}{4}\) , or \(x =\frac{8\pm \sqrt{120}}{4}\) . It''s given that one solution to the equation \(2x^{2}- 8x - 7 = 0\) can be written as \(\frac{8-\sqrt{k}}{4}\) . The solution \(\frac{8-\sqrt{120}}{4}\) is in this form. Therefore, the value of \(k\) is 120.</p>' WHERE id='fada6b03';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p>\(\sqrt[5]{70n}\left(\sqrt[6]{70n}\right)^{2}\)</p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>For what value of \(x\) is the given expression equivalent to \((70n)^{30x}\), where \(n &gt; 1\)?</p>', choices_json='[]', correct_answer='[".0177, .0178, 4/225"]', explanation_html='<p>The correct answer is \(\frac{4}{225}\) . An expression of the form \(k\sqrt{a}\), where \(k\) is an integer greater than 1 and \(a \ge 0\), is equivalent to \(a\frac{1}{k}\) . Therefore, the 1 1 2 given expression, where \(n &gt; 1\), is equivalent to \((70n)5((70n)6)\) . Applying properties of exponents, this expression can be rewritten as 1 1 1 1 1 1 8 2</p><ul><li>\((70n)5(70n)6.^{2}\), or \((70n)5(70n)3\) , which can be rewritten as \((70n)5+3\) , or \((70n)^{1}5\) . It''s given that the expression \(5\sqrt{70n}(^{6}\sqrt{70n})\) is 8 equivalent to \((70n)^{30x}\) , where \(n &gt; 1\). It follows that \((70n)^{1}5\) is equivalent to \((70n)^{30x}\). Therefore, \(\frac{8}{15}= 30x\). Dividing both sides of this equation by 30 yields \(\frac{8}{450}= x\), or \(\frac{4}{225}= x\). Thus, the value of \(x\) for which the given expression is equivalent to \((70n)^{30x}\), where \(n &gt; 1\), is \(\frac{4}{225}\) . Note that 4/225, .0177, .0178, 0.017, and 0.018 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</li></ul>' WHERE id='137cc6fd';
UPDATE questions SET stem_html='<h3>Passage</h3><p><img class="minl" src="/qimg/a520ba07_m6.webp" alt=""></p><h3>Prompt</h3><p>Which of the following expressions is equivalent to the expression above?</p>', choices_json='[{"letter": "A", "content": "<p><img class=\"minl\" src=\"/qimg/a520ba07_m0.webp\" alt=\"\"></p>"}, {"letter": "B", "content": "<p><img class=\"minl\" src=\"/qimg/a520ba07_m1.webp\" alt=\"\"></p>"}, {"letter": "C", "content": "<p><img class=\"minl\" src=\"/qimg/a520ba07_m2.webp\" alt=\"\"></p>"}, {"letter": "D", "content": "<p><img class=\"minl\" src=\"/qimg/a520ba07_m3.webp\" alt=\"\"></p>"}]', correct_answer='["B"]', explanation_html='<p>Choice B is correct. One of the properties of radicals is <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a520ba07_m4.webp" alt="">. Thus, the given expression can be rewritten as <img class="minl" src="/qimg/a520ba07_m5.webp" alt="">.</p><p>Simplifying by taking the cube root of each part gives x1  y2, or xy2.</p><p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect and may be the result of incorrect application of the properties of exponents and radicals.</p>' WHERE id='a520ba07';
